const DECK = {"title":"ECON 380 · Intermediate Micro Theory","weeks":[{"id":"cons","title":"Consumer theory","color":"#1F4E8C","soft":"rgba(31,78,140,0.11)"},{"id":"prod","title":"Production & cost","color":"#138A7E","soft":"rgba(19,138,126,0.11)"},{"id":"mkt","title":"Markets & welfare","color":"#C25A16","soft":"rgba(194,90,22,0.11)"},{"id":"power","title":"Monopoly","color":"#6A4BA8","soft":"rgba(106,75,168,0.11)"},{"id":"game","title":"Game theory","color":"#2E7D4F","soft":"rgba(46,125,79,0.11)"}],"cards":[{"id":"axioms","n":1,"week":1,"term":"Preference axioms","simple":"Rational preferences are <b>complete</b> (any two bundles can be ranked) and <b>transitive</b>; we usually also assume more is better (monotone) and averages are preferred to extremes (convex).","formula":"<i>A</i> ≿ <i>B</i> and <i>B</i> ≿ <i>C</i><span class=\"op\">⇒</span><i>A</i> ≿ <i>C</i>","more":["Completeness and transitivity are what let a <b>utility function</b> represent preferences: give every bundle a number, and the ranking can never loop. Monotonicity makes indifference curves slope down, with higher curves better; convexity bows them toward the origin, which gives a diminishing MRS.","Utility is <b>ordinal</b>: any increasing transformation (√U, ln U, 2U + 7) represents the same preferences and predicts the same choices. Only the ranking matters, so “U = 16 is twice as happy as U = 8” means nothing.","Exam traps: indifference curves can't cross (if they did, transitivity would make one bundle both better than and as good as another). “Convex preferences” means the set of bundles at least as good is convex, so the curve bows <i>toward</i> the origin."],"connections":["mrs","tangency","pareto"],"example":"Someone who prefers Coke to Pepsi, Pepsi to Sprite and Sprite to Coke can be exploited: a vending-machine owner could charge a small fee for each swap and walk them round the loop forever. Transitivity rules this out.","played":["An intransitive shopper prefers A to B, B to C and C to A, and will pay $1 to trade up each time.","Start with C. Pay $1 to swap to B, $1 more for A, and $1 more to get C back.","After 3 trades: the same bundle C, and $3 poorer. This “money pump” is why we assume transitivity."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Budget","args":{"mode":"ic"}},"starred":false},{"id":"mrs","n":2,"week":1,"term":"Marginal rate of substitution (MRS)","simple":"The MRS is how much y the consumer would give up for one more unit of x and stay just as happy: the absolute slope of the indifference curve.","formula":"MRS<span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>MU<sub>x</sub></i></span><span><i>MU<sub>y</sub></i></span></span><span class=\"op\">=</span>−<span class=\"fr\"><span><i>dy</i></span><span><i>dx</i></span></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span>(<i>U</i> fixed)","more":["Why a ratio of marginal utilities: moving along an indifference curve utility doesn't change, so MU<sub>x</sub>·dx + MU<sub>y</sub>·dy = 0, which gives −dy/dx = MU<sub>x</sub>/MU<sub>y</sub>.","<b>Diminishing MRS</b> (convex preferences): the more x you already have, the less y you'll give up for another unit. For Cobb–Douglas x<sup>a</sup>y<sup>1−a</sup>, MRS = (a/(1 − a))·(y/x), which falls as x rises.","Traps: with x on the horizontal axis, MU<sub>x</sub> goes on top (y given up per unit of x). Perfect substitutes have a constant MRS (straight-line indifference curves); perfect complements have none at the kink."],"connections":["axioms","tangency","cddemand","mrts"],"example":"A commuter who is just indifferent between a $3 express bus and a free local bus that takes 20 minutes longer is trading time for money at $0.15 a minute. That trade-off (their MRS between time and money) is what transport planners call the value of time.","played":["Running example U = x<sup>0.5</sup>y<sup>0.5</sup>: MU<sub>x</sub> = 0.5(y/x)<sup>0.5</sup>, MU<sub>y</sub> = 0.5(x/y)<sup>0.5</sup>, so MRS = y/x.","At (x, y) = (25, 10): MRS = 10/25 = 0.4. One more x is worth 0.4 units of y.","At (10, 25), on the same curve (U = 15.8): MRS = 25/10 = 2.5. When x is scarce each unit is worth far more y: diminishing MRS."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Budget","args":{"mode":"mrs"}},"starred":false},{"id":"tangency","n":3,"week":1,"term":"Utility-max condition","simple":"At an interior optimum the consumer spends all income on the bundle where the indifference curve is tangent to the budget line, so the MRS equals the price ratio.","formula":"MRS<span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>p<sub>x</sub></i></span><span><i>p<sub>y</sub></i></span></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span><i>p<sub>x</sub>x</i><span class=\"op\">+</span><i>p<sub>y</sub>y</i><span class=\"op\">=</span><i>I</i>","more":["Intuition: the MRS is what the consumer is <i>willing</i> to trade; p<sub>x</sub>/p<sub>y</sub> is what the market <i>lets</i> them trade. If MRS &gt; p<sub>x</sub>/p<sub>y</sub>, x is worth more than it costs in y, so buy more x. At the optimum the two match.","Equivalent “bang for the buck” form: MU<sub>x</sub>/p<sub>x</sub> = MU<sub>y</sub>/p<sub>y</sub>. The last dollar spent on each good adds the same utility, and that common value is λ.","Traps: tangency is the rule only for an <b>interior</b> solution with smooth, convex preferences. Perfect substitutes give corners (spend everything on the good with more MU per dollar); perfect complements give a kink. And always use the budget line too: two equations, two unknowns."],"connections":["mrs","cddemand","lagrange","costmin"],"example":"When a streaming service raises its monthly price, a subscriber who was just at the tangency now finds the last dollar on streaming buys less enjoyment than a dollar on other things, so some cancel or move to a cheaper ad-supported tier.","played":["Running example: U = x<sup>0.5</sup>y<sup>0.5</sup>, I = 100, p<sub>x</sub> = 2, p<sub>y</sub> = 5.","Tangency: y/x = 2/5, so y = 0.4x. Budget: 2x + 5(0.4x) = 4x = 100, so x = 25, y = 10.","Compare an affordable bundle off the tangency, (40, 4): its MRS is 0.1 &lt; 0.4 and U = √160 = 12.6 &lt; 15.8. Shifting spending back toward y raises utility."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Budget","args":{"mode":"opt"}},"starred":false},{"id":"cddemand","n":4,"week":1,"term":"Cobb–Douglas demands","simple":"With Cobb–Douglas utility the consumer always spends the fixed share a of income on x and the share 1 − a on y.","formula":"<i>U</i><span class=\"op\">=</span><i>x<sup>a</sup>y</i><sup>1−<i>a</i></sup><span class=\"op\">⇒</span><i>x</i>*<span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>aI</i></span><span><i>p<sub>x</sub></i></span></span>, <i>y</i>*<span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span>(1 − <i>a</i>)<i>I</i></span><span><i>p<sub>y</sub></i></span></span>","more":["Derivation: tangency (a/(1 − a))(y/x) = p<sub>x</sub>/p<sub>y</sub> gives p<sub>y</sub>y = ((1 − a)/a)p<sub>x</sub>x. Put that in the budget and p<sub>x</sub>x = aI.","Consequences: demand for x doesn't depend on p<sub>y</sub> (no cross-price effect), its own-price elasticity is exactly −1 (spending on x stays aI whatever its price), and its income elasticity is exactly 1. Both goods are normal; neither can be Giffen.","Trap: if the exponents don't add to 1 (U = x<sup>2</sup>y<sup>3</sup>), the shares are a/(a + b) and b/(a + b), here 2/5 and 3/5. Normalise first."],"connections":["tangency","elas","goods","slutsky"],"example":"Cobb–Douglas is a reasonable first guess for broad budget categories whose shares are fairly stable, such as housing taking roughly a third of US household spending. It fails for necessities like food, whose budget share falls as income rises (Engel's law).","played":["U = x<sup>0.5</sup>y<sup>0.5</sup>, I = 100, p<sub>x</sub> = 2, p<sub>y</sub> = 5.","x* = 0.5 × 100 / 2 = 25; y* = 0.5 × 100 / 5 = 10. Half the budget ($50) goes on each good.","Income doubles to 200: x* = 50, y* = 20. If instead p<sub>y</sub> doubles to 10: x* stays 25 and y* falls to 5."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Budget","args":{"mode":"income"}},"starred":false},{"id":"lagrange","n":5,"week":1,"term":"Lagrange multiplier λ","simple":"In max U(x, y) subject to the budget, the multiplier λ at the optimum is the marginal utility of income: how much maximised utility rises per extra dollar.","formula":"ℒ<span class=\"op\">=</span><i>U</i>(<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>)<span class=\"op\">+</span>λ(<i>I</i> − <i>p<sub>x</sub>x</i> − <i>p<sub>y</sub>y</i>)<span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span>λ*<span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>MU<sub>x</sub></i></span><span><i>p<sub>x</sub></i></span></span><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span>∂<i>U</i>*</span><span>∂<i>I</i></span></span>","more":["First-order conditions: MU<sub>x</sub> = λp<sub>x</sub>, MU<sub>y</sub> = λp<sub>y</sub>, and the budget. Dividing the first two gives the tangency MRS = p<sub>x</sub>/p<sub>y</sub>; the budget then says where on the line.","λ is a <b>shadow price</b>: the value of loosening the constraint by one unit. The same idea appears in cost minimisation, where the multiplier on the output constraint is marginal cost.","Trap: because utility is ordinal, the size of λ depends on which utility function you picked. Its role in the conditions is meaningful; comparing its level across people is not."],"connections":["tangency","hicks","costmin"],"example":"An extra $100 rebate matters far more to a household living paycheck to paycheck than to a very wealthy one. That idea, a falling marginal utility of income, is behind arguments for progressive transfers, though comparing λ across people needs assumptions beyond ordinal utility.","played":["Running example at (25, 10): MU<sub>x</sub> = 0.5√(10/25) = 0.5√0.4 = 0.316.","λ = MU<sub>x</sub>/p<sub>x</sub> = 0.316/2 = 0.158. Check: MU<sub>y</sub>/p<sub>y</sub> = 0.5√2.5 / 5 = 0.791/5 = 0.158.","Raise I to 101: x = 25.25, y = 10.1, U = √255.025 = 15.970, up 0.158 from 15.811. λ predicted it."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Budget","args":{"mode":"opt"}},"starred":false},{"id":"hicks","n":6,"week":1,"term":"Marshallian vs Hicksian demand","simple":"Marshallian demand x(p, I) is what a consumer buys with a fixed income; Hicksian (compensated) demand h(p, U) is the cheapest bundle that keeps utility at U, so it shows only the substitution effect.","formula":"<i>h<sub>x</sub></i>(<i>p</i>, <i>U</i>)<span class=\"op\">=</span><i>x</i>(<i>p</i>, <i>e</i>(<i>p</i>, <i>U</i>))","more":["Marshallian demand comes from maximising utility; Hicksian from <b>minimising expenditure</b> (min p<sub>x</sub>x + p<sub>y</sub>y subject to U(x, y) = U). The minimum spending, e(p, U), is the expenditure function. At the starting prices and utility the two demands give the same bundle.","Hicksian demand always slopes down. For a <b>normal</b> good the Marshallian curve is more price-responsive (flatter in the usual P-on-the-vertical picture), because the income effect adds to the substitution effect; for an inferior good it's less responsive.","Uses: the Slutsky equation and exact welfare measures (compensating variation is the area to the left of the Hicksian curve). Trap: you can't see Hicksian demand in market data; you get it by compensating income."],"connections":["slutsky","lagrange","goods","elas"],"example":"Indexing Social Security benefits to the CPI is an attempt at Hicksian compensation: raise income after prices rise so retirees can stay as well off. A fixed-basket index overcompensates because it ignores that people substitute away from goods that got dearer.","played":["Running example, and p<sub>x</sub> rises from 2 to 8 (p<sub>y</sub> = 5, I = 100).","Marshallian: x = 0.5 × 100 / 8 = 6.25 (from 25).","Hicksian at U = 15.81: x = U√(p<sub>y</sub>/p<sub>x</sub>) = 15.81 × √(5/8) = 12.5, with y = 20, costing 8(12.5) + 5(20) = $200.","The compensated fall (25 → 12.5) is smaller than the ordinary fall (25 → 6.25): x is a normal good."],"graph":"econ380Demand","starred":false},{"id":"slutsky","n":7,"week":1,"term":"Slutsky equation","simple":"The Slutsky equation splits the effect of a price change on demand into a substitution effect (never positive) and an income effect from the change in purchasing power.","formula":"<span class=\"fr\"><span>∂<i>x</i></span><span>∂<i>p<sub>x</sub></i></span></span><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span>∂<i>h</i></span><span>∂<i>p<sub>x</sub></i></span></span><span class=\"op\">−</span><i>x</i><span class=\"fr\"><span>∂<i>x</i></span><span>∂<i>I</i></span></span>","more":["Read it in two parts: a higher p<sub>x</sub> makes x relatively dearer (substitution, ∂h/∂p<sub>x</sub> ≤ 0) and makes you poorer by about x dollars per $1 rise (income, −x·∂x/∂I). For a normal good both parts are negative.","On a graph (A → B → C): A is the old optimum; B is where the old indifference curve touches a line at the new price ratio (Hicks), or the optimum when you can just afford A (Slutsky compensation); C is the new optimum. A → B is substitution, B → C is income.","Traps: the income effect is weighted by x, so it matters most for goods with a big budget share. A Giffen good needs an inferior good <i>and</i> a big budget share. Hicks and Slutsky compensation split a large price change slightly differently; for small changes they agree."],"connections":["hicks","goods","cddemand","mrs"],"example":"When US gasoline prices spiked in 2022, drivers cut back on trips and some shifted toward more fuel-efficient cars (substitution), while families with long commutes also cut other spending because their real income fell (income effect).","played":["Running example at p<sub>x</sub> = 2: x = 25 and ∂x/∂p<sub>x</sub> = −aI/p<sub>x</sub><sup>2</sup> = −50/4 = −12.5.","Substitution: ∂h/∂p<sub>x</sub> = −0.5U√p<sub>y</sub>/p<sub>x</sub><sup>1.5</sup> = −0.5 × 15.81 × 2.236 / 2.828 = −6.25.","Income: x·∂x/∂I = 25 × 0.5/2 = 6.25, entering with a minus sign: −6.25 − 6.25 = −12.5 ✓.","For the big jump 2 → 8 (the graph): substitution 25 → 12.5, income 12.5 → 6.25."],"graph":"econ380PriceEffects","starred":false},{"id":"goods","n":8,"week":1,"term":"Normal, inferior, Giffen","simple":"A normal good is bought more when income rises and an inferior good less; a Giffen good is an inferior good whose income effect is strong enough that a price rise increases the quantity demanded.","formula":"η<span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span>∂<i>x</i></span><span>∂<i>I</i></span></span>·<span class=\"fr\"><span><i>I</i></span><span><i>x</i></span></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span>normal: η &gt; 0, inferior: η &lt; 0","more":["Within normal goods, <b>necessities</b> have 0 &lt; η &lt; 1 (their budget share falls as income rises) and <b>luxuries</b> have η &gt; 1.","Giffen needs three things: an inferior good, a large budget share (so the income effect is big) and few substitutes (so the substitution effect is small). Every Giffen good is inferior, but almost no inferior goods are Giffen.","Traps: “inferior” is about income, not quality, and it depends on the income range: a good can be normal for the very poor and inferior for the middle class. No good can be inferior at every income, since spending has to rise from zero somewhere."],"connections":["slutsky","hicks","cddemand","elas"],"example":"Jensen and Miller subsidised rice for very poor households in Hunan, China, and found Giffen behaviour: many bought <i>less</i> rice when it got cheaper, using the freed-up income for more appealing foods. Instant noodles and long-distance bus travel are classic inferior goods.","numbers":[["Share of after-tax income spent on food, lowest income fifth, 2023","32.6%"],["Same, highest income fifth, 2023","8.1%"]],"numbersSource":"Sources: USDA Economic Research Service, Charts of Note (2023 data; ERS has since moved to before-tax income); Jensen & Miller, “Giffen Behavior and Subsistence Consumption”, American Economic Review (2008). Food is normal but a necessity: richer households spend more dollars but a smaller share.","played":["Running example x = 0.5I/p<sub>x</sub>: income 100 → 120 raises x from 25 to 30. Normal.","η = (Δx/x)/(ΔI/I) = (5/25)/(20/100) = 0.2/0.2 = 1. Cobb–Douglas goods have unit income elasticity.","Contrast an inferior good: a student buys 20 packs of instant noodles a month on $2,000, but 16 on $2,500. η = (−20%)/(25%) = −0.8."],"graph":"econ380Engel","starred":false},{"id":"elas","n":9,"week":1,"term":"Price elasticity of demand","simple":"The price elasticity of demand is the percentage change in quantity demanded for a 1% change in price; demand is elastic if |ε| &gt; 1 and inelastic if |ε| &lt; 1.","formula":"ε<span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span>∂<i>Q</i></span><span>∂<i>P</i></span></span>·<span class=\"fr\"><span><i>P</i></span><span><i>Q</i></span></span>","more":["Revenue rule: when demand is elastic, a price cut raises revenue; when inelastic, it lowers it. Revenue peaks where |ε| = 1, which is where MR = 0.","On a <b>linear</b> demand curve the slope is constant but the elasticity isn't: |ε| = (1/b)(P/Q) runs from ∞ at the price intercept, through 1 at the midpoint, to 0 at the quantity intercept. Don't read “steep” as “inelastic” unless you compare two curves at the same point.","Demand is more elastic with close substitutes, a large budget share, a narrowly defined good (Shell gasoline rather than gasoline) and more time to adjust."],"connections":["lerner","incidence","mrmc","cddemand"],"example":"When gasoline prices jump, drivers barely cut back in the first months: they still have to get to work. Over years they buy more efficient cars and move closer to work, so long-run demand is several times more elastic.","numbers":[["Short-run price elasticity of US gasoline demand, 2001–06","−0.034 to −0.077"],["Same, 1975–80","−0.21 to −0.34"]],"numbersSource":"Source: Hughes, Knittel & Sperling, “Evidence of a Shift in the Short-Run Price Elasticity of Gasoline Demand”, The Energy Journal 29(1), 2008.","played":["Market demand P = 100 − 2Q, so Q = 50 − P/2 and dQ/dP = −0.5. At P = 40, Q = 30.","ε = −0.5 × 40/30 = −0.67: inelastic. Raise P to 42: Q = 29, revenue 1,200 → 1,218. Up.","At P = 60, Q = 20: ε = −0.5 × 60/20 = −1.5: elastic. Raise P to 62: Q = 19, revenue 1,200 → 1,178. Down.","Unit elastic at the midpoint P = 50, Q = 25, where revenue peaks at 1,250."],"graph":"econ380Elasticity","starred":false},{"id":"rts","n":10,"week":2,"term":"Returns to scale","simple":"Returns to scale describe what happens to output when every input is scaled up by the same factor: output rises more than proportionally (increasing), proportionally (constant) or less (decreasing).","formula":"<i>F</i>(<i>tK</i>, <i>tL</i>) vs <i>t</i>·<i>F</i>(<i>K</i>, <i>L</i>)<span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span><i>K</i><sup>α</sup><i>L</i><sup>β</sup>: compare α + β with 1","more":["Returns to scale is a long-run, all-inputs-together idea. Diminishing <b>marginal</b> product is one input at a time. A function can have diminishing MP<sub>L</sub> and MP<sub>K</sub> and still have increasing returns to scale (K<sup>0.6</sup>L<sup>0.6</sup>).","Returns to scale shape long-run average cost: with fixed input prices, increasing returns mean falling average cost (economies of scale), constant returns flat, decreasing returns rising.","Traps: for Cobb–Douglas, add the exponents; don't judge each one alone. And returns can differ by scale: increasing for a small plant, decreasing for a huge one."],"connections":["mrts","costmin","lrcomp","mcac"],"example":"Pipelines show increasing returns: doubling a pipe's diameter roughly quadruples its cross-section (what it can carry) but only about doubles the steel around it. That geometry is one reason oil and gas pipelines tend toward natural monopoly.","played":["Running example Q = K<sup>0.5</sup>L<sup>0.5</sup> at K = 40, L = 10: Q = √400 = 20.","Double both: K = 80, L = 20, so Q = √1,600 = 40. Exactly double: constant returns (0.5 + 0.5 = 1).","Compare Q = K<sup>0.3</sup>L<sup>0.8</sup>: doubling both inputs multiplies output by 2<sup>1.1</sup> = 2.14 &gt; 2. Increasing returns."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Isoquant","args":{"mode":"scale"}},"starred":false},{"id":"mrts","n":11,"week":2,"term":"MRTS","simple":"The marginal rate of technical substitution is how much capital the firm can give up for one more unit of labor while keeping output the same: the absolute slope of the isoquant.","formula":"MRTS<span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>MP<sub>L</sub></i></span><span><i>MP<sub>K</sub></i></span></span><span class=\"op\">=</span>−<span class=\"fr\"><span><i>dK</i></span><span><i>dL</i></span></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span>(<i>Q</i> fixed)","more":["Same algebra as the MRS: along an isoquant MP<sub>L</sub>·dL + MP<sub>K</sub>·dK = 0. Diminishing MRTS (convex isoquants) means each extra worker replaces less and less capital.","Unlike utility, output is <b>cardinal</b>: the Q = 40 isoquant really is twice the output of Q = 20.","Shapes: perfect substitutes (straight isoquants, constant MRTS), fixed proportions (L-shaped: one driver per truck), Cobb–Douglas in between."],"connections":["mrs","costmin","rts"],"example":"A supermarket can run checkout with more cashiers or more self-checkout machines. In a store that is already mostly self-checkout, one more cashier frees up few machines; in a store with almost none, a cashier replaces a lot.","played":["Q = K<sup>0.5</sup>L<sup>0.5</sup>: MP<sub>L</sub> = 0.5√(K/L), MP<sub>K</sub> = 0.5√(L/K), so MRTS = K/L.","At (L, K) = (10, 40), Q = 20: MRTS = 4. One more worker replaces 4 units of capital.","At (20, 20), on the same isoquant: MRTS = 1. Diminishing MRTS."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Isoquant","args":{"mode":"mrts"}},"starred":false},{"id":"costmin","n":12,"week":2,"term":"Cost-minimization condition","simple":"To make a given output at the lowest cost, the firm picks the inputs where the isoquant is tangent to an isocost line, so the MRTS equals the input price ratio w/r.","formula":"<span class=\"fr\"><span><i>MP<sub>L</sub></i></span><span><i>MP<sub>K</sub></i></span></span><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>w</i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span><span class=\"op\">⇔</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>MP<sub>L</sub></i></span><span><i>w</i></span></span><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>MP<sub>K</sub></i></span><span><i>r</i></span></span>","more":["Intuition: if MP<sub>L</sub>/w &gt; MP<sub>K</sub>/r, a dollar moved from capital to labor buys more output, so keep switching until output per dollar is equal for both inputs.","Doing this for every output level gives the cost function C(Q) and the conditional input demands L(w, r, Q), K(w, r, Q). The Lagrange multiplier on the output constraint is marginal cost.","Traps: cost minimisation is only the first step of profit maximisation; a firm can make the wrong quantity at least cost. A rise in w makes the firm substitute toward capital <i>for a given Q</i>."],"connections":["mrts","tangency","lagrange","mcac"],"example":"Fast-food chains such as McDonald's have installed self-order kiosks. Whenever wages rise relative to the cost of machines, the cost-minimising mix shifts toward kiosks and away from counter staff; where labor is cheap, staffed counters stay cheaper.","played":["Running example: Q = K<sup>0.5</sup>L<sup>0.5</sup>, w = 4, r = 1, target Q = 20.","Tangency: K/L = w/r = 4, so K = 4L. Then Q = √(4L<sup>2</sup>) = 2L = 20, so L = 10, K = 40.","Cost = 4(10) + 1(40) = $80, an average cost of $4 a unit. Check (L, K) = (20, 20), also Q = 20: cost 4(20) + 20 = $100. Dearer."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Isoquant","args":{"mode":"opt"}},"starred":false},{"id":"mcac","n":13,"week":2,"term":"MC crosses AC at its minimum","simple":"When marginal cost is below average cost, average cost falls, and when it is above, average cost rises, so the MC curve crosses AVC and ATC at their lowest points.","formula":"ATC<span class=\"op\">=</span>AVC<span class=\"op\">+</span><span class=\"fr\"><span>FC</span><span><i>q</i></span></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>d</i>AC</span><span><i>dq</i></span></span><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span>MC − AC</span><span><i>q</i></span></span>","more":["Grade analogy: if your next exam score (the marginal one) is below your average, your average falls. It's the same arithmetic.","Short-run shapes: average fixed cost FC/q falls forever; AVC and ATC are U-shaped; the gap between ATC and AVC is AFC, which shrinks as q grows. Fixed cost doesn't affect MC at all.","Drawing rule for exams: MC passes through the <b>bottom</b> of AVC first and then the bottom of ATC (min ATC is at a larger q than min AVC). Label every curve and both minimum points."],"connections":["shutdown","lrcomp","costmin","rts"],"example":"An airline's planes and gates are paid for whether a flight is full or not, so average cost per passenger falls as seats fill, while the marginal cost of one more passenger on a half-empty flight is little more than a snack and a bit of fuel.","played":["Running firm: TC = 32 + q<sup>3</sup> − 6q<sup>2</sup> + 20q, so MC = 3q<sup>2</sup> − 12q + 20 and AVC = q<sup>2</sup> − 6q + 20.","Min AVC at q = 3: AVC = 9 − 18 + 20 = 11, and MC(3) = 27 − 36 + 20 = 11. Equal.","Min ATC at q = 4: ATC = 16 − 24 + 20 + 32/4 = 20, and MC(4) = 48 − 48 + 20 = 20. Equal."],"graph":"econ380Costs","starred":false},{"id":"shutdown","n":14,"week":2,"term":"Short-run shutdown rule","simple":"In the short run a competitive firm produces where P = MC as long as price covers minimum average variable cost; below that it shuts down and loses only its fixed cost.","formula":"produce where <i>P</i><span class=\"op\">=</span>MC if <i>P</i><span class=\"op\">≥</span>min AVC; otherwise <i>q</i><span class=\"op\">=</span>0","more":["Fixed cost is sunk in the short run: it's paid whether or not the firm produces, so it shouldn't affect the decision. What matters is whether revenue covers <b>variable</b> cost.","So the short-run supply curve is the MC curve above min AVC. Between min AVC and min ATC the firm produces at a loss, but a smaller one than shutting down. In the long run (no fixed costs) it exits if P &lt; min ATC.","Trap: “shut down” (produce nothing for now, still pay fixed cost) is not “exit” (leave the industry and pay nothing)."],"connections":["mcac","lrcomp","csps"],"example":"During the 2020 COVID-19 lockdowns many restaurants closed their dining rooms for months while still paying rent: takeout sales wouldn't cover staff and food (variable costs), and the lease (fixed) had to be paid either way.","played":["Running firm: min AVC = 11 (q = 3), min ATC = 20 (q = 4), fixed cost 32.","P = 35: MC = 35 at q = 5. Revenue 175, cost 32 + 125 − 150 + 100 = 107, profit 68.","P = 14.75: MC = 14.75 at q = 3.5. Revenue 51.6, cost 71.4: a loss of 19.75, less than the 32 lost by shutting. Stay open.","P = 8 &lt; 11: at q = 2, AVC = 12 &gt; 8, so producing loses 32 + 8 = 40. Shut down and lose 32."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Costs","args":{"mode":"shutdown"}},"starred":false},{"id":"lrcomp","n":15,"week":2,"term":"Long-run competitive equilibrium","simple":"In the long run, free entry and exit push price to the minimum of average cost, so each firm earns zero economic profit and produces at its efficient scale.","formula":"<i>P</i><span class=\"op\">=</span>MC<span class=\"op\">=</span>min ATC","more":["Positive profit draws entrants, shifting market supply right and pulling P down; losses cause exit, pushing P up. Movement stops only where P = min ATC.","Zero <b>economic</b> profit still means a normal return: opportunity costs, including the owner's time and capital, are already counted as costs.","With identical firms and constant input prices the long-run supply curve is flat at min ATC and the number of firms adjusts to demand. If input prices rise as the industry grows, long-run supply slopes up (an increasing-cost industry)."],"connections":["mcac","shutdown","fwt","rts"],"example":"When bubble tea became popular in US cities, early shops earned high profits. New shops opened on many blocks, and prices and profits fell back toward normal.","played":["Running firm: min ATC = 20 at q = 4, so the long-run price is $20.","Market demand Q = 1,000 − 20P: at P = 20, Q = 600.","Number of firms = 600 / 4 = 150, each earning (20 − 20) × 4 = 0.","Demand rises to Q = 1,200 − 20P: price and profit rise for a while; firms enter until P = 20 again, now 800 / 4 = 200 firms."],"graph":"econ380LongRun","starred":false},{"id":"csps","n":16,"week":3,"term":"Consumer & producer surplus","simple":"Consumer surplus is what buyers were willing to pay minus what they paid (the area under demand, above the price); producer surplus is what sellers received minus their marginal cost (the area above supply, below the price).","formula":"CS<span class=\"op\">=</span>∫(<i>D</i>(<i>Q</i>) − <i>P</i>)<i>dQ</i><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span>linear: ½ × base × height","more":["Each point on the demand curve is some buyer's willingness to pay; each point on supply is some seller's marginal cost. Surplus adds up the gain on every unit traded.","Total surplus (CS + PS) is largest at the competitive equilibrium: every trade worth more than it costs happens, and none worth less.","Traps: PS is not profit (it ignores fixed cost). With linear curves use triangles, but check where the curves meet the axes. With a tax, the buyers' price bounds CS and the sellers' price bounds PS."],"connections":["dwl","incidence","fwt","mrmc"],"example":"Uber's surge pricing let economists see how ridership responded to price at many points along the demand curve, and so estimate how much more riders would have paid than they actually did.","numbers":[["Consumer surplus from UberX, 4 US cities, 2015","≈ $2.9 billion"],["Consumer surplus per $1 spent","≈ $1.60"]],"numbersSource":"Source: Cohen, Hahn, Hall, Levitt & Metcalfe, “Using Big Data to Estimate Consumer Surplus: The Case of Uber”, NBER Working Paper 22627 (2016). Two of the authors worked at Uber.","played":["Running market: demand P = 100 − 2Q, supply P = 10 + Q. 100 − 2Q = 10 + Q, so Q = 30 and P = 40.","CS = ½ × 30 × (100 − 40) = 900.","PS = ½ × 30 × (40 − 10) = 450. Total surplus = 1,350."],"graph":"econ380Surplus","starred":false},{"id":"dwl","n":17,"week":3,"term":"Deadweight loss","simple":"Deadweight loss is the total surplus lost compared with the efficient outcome: trades that would benefit both sides but don't happen (or wasteful trades that do).","formula":"DWL<span class=\"op\">≈</span>½ × <i>t</i> × Δ<i>Q</i><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span>(linear curves)","more":["Nobody collects it. Tax revenue goes to the government, so it isn't part of the DWL; the DWL triangle is the value of the trades the tax prevented.","DWL grows with the <b>square</b> of the tax: doubling a tax roughly quadruples it. That is the case for broad tax bases with low rates. It's also bigger when supply and demand are more elastic, because quantity responds more.","Sources: taxes, subsidies (too much is produced), price controls, monopoly, externalities. Trap: a subsidy creates DWL too, even though buyers and sellers both seem to gain."],"connections":["incidence","csps","ceiling","mrmc"],"example":"England's window tax (1696–1851) charged houses by the number of windows. Owners bricked windows up: they lost light and air, and the Treasury collected nothing from the windows that vanished. That loss is pure deadweight.","played":["Running market with a $6 per-unit tax: 100 − 2Q = 10 + Q + 6, so Q = 28 (from 30).","DWL = ½ × 6 × (30 − 28) = 6.","Check the accounts: CS = 784, PS = 392, revenue = 6 × 28 = 168. Total 1,344, against 1,350 before: 6 lost.","Double the tax to $12: Q = 26 and DWL = ½ × 12 × 4 = 24, four times as large."],"graph":"econ380Tax","starred":false},{"id":"incidence","n":18,"week":3,"term":"Tax incidence","simple":"Tax incidence is who actually bears a tax; it depends on elasticities, not on who legally pays, and the less elastic side bears more.","formula":"buyers' share<span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span>ε<sub>S</sub></span><span>ε<sub>S</sub> + |ε<sub>D</sub>|</span></span>","more":["A per-unit tax drives a wedge t between the price buyers pay and the price sellers keep: P<sub>b</sub> − P<sub>s</sub> = t. Whether the government collects it from buyers or from sellers, the same P<sub>b</sub>, P<sub>s</sub> and Q result.","With linear curves, the buyers' share is also (demand slope)/(demand slope + supply slope), with slopes measured as ΔP per unit of Q: the steeper side pays more.","Extremes: perfectly inelastic demand or perfectly elastic supply means buyers pay it all; perfectly inelastic supply (land) means sellers pay it all."],"connections":["dwl","elas","csps","ceiling"],"example":"US payroll taxes are split by law half on workers and half on employers, but because labor supply is fairly inelastic, economists generally think workers bear much of the employer's half too, through lower wages. State gasoline taxes show up almost one-for-one in pump prices.","numbers":[["Federal gasoline excise tax (unchanged since 1993)","18.4¢ a gallon"],["Average pass-through of state gasoline taxes to pump prices","≈ 100%"],["Payroll tax on each of employer and employee: Social Security + Medicare (2025)","6.2% + 1.45%"]],"numbersSource":"Sources: Tax Policy Center (federal gas tax); Marion & Muehlegger, “Fuel Tax Incidence and Supply Conditions”, Journal of Public Economics (2011); IRS/SSA 2025 payroll tax rates.","played":["Running market, $6 tax: Q = 28, P<sub>b</sub> = 100 − 56 = 44, P<sub>s</sub> = 44 − 6 = 38 (from 40).","Buyers pay 4 of the 6 (67%); sellers bear 2 (33%).","Elasticities at the old equilibrium: |ε<sub>D</sub>| = 0.5 × 40/30 = 0.67, ε<sub>S</sub> = 1 × 40/30 = 1.33. Buyers' share = 1.33/(1.33 + 0.67) = 0.67 ✓."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Tax","args":{"mode":"incidence"}},"starred":false},{"id":"ceiling","n":19,"week":3,"term":"Binding price ceiling","simple":"A binding price ceiling is a legal maximum price set below the equilibrium price; quantity falls to what sellers will supply, leaving a shortage and a deadweight loss.","formula":"<i>Q</i><span class=\"op\">=</span><i>Q<sub>S</sub></i>(<i>P̄</i>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;</span><i>Q<sub>D</sub></i>(<i>P̄</i>)<span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span>shortage<span class=\"op\">=</span><i>Q<sub>D</sub></i> − <i>Q<sub>S</sub></i>","more":["It only binds if it's set <b>below</b> the equilibrium price; a ceiling above it does nothing. (A price floor binds only above equilibrium.)","The textbook DWL assumes the scarce units go to the buyers who value them most. In practice they go to whoever queues, searches longest or knows the landlord, so the real losses are larger: time spent searching, black markets, quality cuts.","Who gains: buyers who still get the good, at a lower price. Who loses: sellers, and buyers squeezed out. Supply is more elastic in the long run, so shortages grow over time."],"connections":["dwl","csps","incidence","pareto"],"example":"Rent control: when San Francisco extended rent control to small older apartment buildings in 1994, covered tenants were more likely to stay put, but landlords converted many covered buildings to condos and owner-occupied housing, shrinking the rental stock.","numbers":[["Fall in rental housing supplied by landlords newly covered by SF rent control","15%"],["Fall in covered tenants' mobility (more stayed put)","≈ 20%"]],"numbersSource":"Source: Diamond, McQuade & Qian, “The Effects of Rent Control Expansion on Tenants, Landlords, and Inequality: Evidence from San Francisco”, American Economic Review (2019).","played":["Running market, ceiling P̄ = $34 (the equilibrium price was $40).","Supply: Q<sub>S</sub> = 34 − 10 = 24. Demand: Q<sub>D</sub> = (100 − 34)/2 = 33. Shortage = 9.","On the 24th unit buyers would pay 100 − 48 = $52, but it costs sellers only $34.","DWL = ½ × (30 − 24) × (52 − 34) = 54, assuming the 24 units go to the buyers who value them most."],"graph":"econ380Ceiling","starred":false},{"id":"pareto","n":20,"week":3,"term":"Pareto efficiency & the contract curve","simple":"An allocation is Pareto efficient if no one can be made better off without making someone else worse off; in an Edgeworth box these are the points where the two people's MRSs are equal, which together form the contract curve.","formula":"MRS<sub>A</sub><span class=\"op\">=</span>MRS<sub>B</sub>","more":["If the MRSs differ, there's a trade both like: A values x more (in units of y) than B does, so B sells x to A at a rate in between. The lens between their indifference curves through the starting point holds all such trades.","The contract curve runs from one origin to the other. Points on it are efficient but can be very unequal, including one person having everything. Efficiency says nothing about fairness.","Trap: a <b>Pareto improvement</b> (someone better off, nobody worse off) is not the same as <b>Pareto efficient</b> (no improvement left). Moving from an inefficient point to some efficient point isn't always an improvement for both."],"connections":["fwt","mrs","axioms","ceiling"],"example":"Swapping lunch items: if you value your friend's cookie more than they do, and they value your apple more than you do, a swap makes both better off. Once no such swaps are left, the lunch allocation is Pareto efficient.","played":["Two people, each U = x<sup>0.5</sup>y<sup>0.5</sup> (MRS = y/x). Totals: 10 of x, 10 of y. A starts with (8, 2), B with (2, 8).","MRS<sub>A</sub> = 2/8 = 0.25, MRS<sub>B</sub> = 8/2 = 4. Unequal, so not efficient: A would give up x for y.","Contract curve: y<sub>A</sub>/x<sub>A</sub> = (10 − y<sub>A</sub>)/(10 − x<sub>A</sub>), so y<sub>A</sub> = x<sub>A</sub>: the diagonal.","Move to (5, 5) each: utility rises from √16 = 4 to 5 for both. A Pareto improvement, onto the contract curve."],"graph":"econ380Edgeworth","starred":false},{"id":"fwt","n":21,"week":3,"term":"First welfare theorem","simple":"The first welfare theorem says that with competitive markets, no externalities and full information, a competitive equilibrium is Pareto efficient.","formula":"MRS<sub>A</sub><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>p<sub>x</sub></i></span><span><i>p<sub>y</sub></i></span></span><span class=\"op\">=</span>MRS<sub>B</sub>","more":["Why: every consumer sets their MRS equal to the price ratio, and they all face the same prices, so every MRS is equal: efficient exchange. Firms set price equal to MC, so what it costs to make more of a good matches what consumers will give up for it.","It's Adam Smith's invisible hand made precise, and it lists exactly when markets fail: market power, externalities, public goods, asymmetric information. Those failures are much of the rest of microeconomics.","The <b>second</b> welfare theorem runs the other way: any efficient allocation can be reached as a competitive equilibrium after suitable lump-sum transfers. In theory, efficiency and equity can be handled separately."],"connections":["pareto","lrcomp","csps","mrmc"],"example":"Wholesale electricity auctions apply the idea: generators bid to supply, the market clears at one price, and power comes from the cheapest plants. When suppliers could exercise market power, as in the 2000–01 California electricity crisis, the theorem's conditions failed and prices spiked.","played":["Edgeworth economy: A has (8, 2), B has (2, 8), both U = x<sup>0.5</sup>y<sup>0.5</sup>. Set p<sub>y</sub> = 1.","Try p<sub>x</sub> = 1. A's wealth = 8 + 2 = 10, so A demands x = 0.5 × 10 / 1 = 5 and y = 5. B's wealth is also 10: B demands (5, 5).","Markets clear: x demanded 5 + 5 = 10 = x available. MRS<sub>A</sub> = MRS<sub>B</sub> = 1 = p<sub>x</sub>/p<sub>y</sub>: on the contract curve. Efficient."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Edgeworth","args":{"mode":"ce"}},"starred":false},{"id":"mrmc","n":22,"week":4,"term":"Monopoly rule","simple":"A monopolist maximises profit by producing where marginal revenue equals marginal cost and charging the highest price the demand curve allows at that quantity.","formula":"MR(<i>Q</i>)<span class=\"op\">=</span>MC(<i>Q</i>)<span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span><i>P</i><span class=\"op\">=</span><i>a</i> − <i>bQ</i><span class=\"op\">⇒</span>MR<span class=\"op\">=</span><i>a</i> − 2<i>bQ</i>","more":["MR is below price because to sell one more unit the monopolist must cut the price on <b>all</b> its units, not just the last one. With linear demand, MR has the same intercept and twice the slope.","Compared with competition: lower Q, higher P, a transfer from consumers to the firm (the profit rectangle) and a deadweight-loss triangle between demand and MC from Q<sub>m</sub> to Q<sub>c</sub>.","Traps: a monopolist has no supply curve (its output depends on the shape of demand, not just the price). Read the price off the <b>demand</b> curve, not MR or MC. It never produces where demand is inelastic, because MR &lt; 0 there."],"connections":["lerner","pricedisc","dwl","elas"],"example":"For years after 2007, Mylan's EpiPen faced little competition in the US, and its list price rose several-fold: a firm with market power pricing far above its marginal cost.","numbers":[["EpiPen two-pack list price, January 2007","$94"],["Same, May 2016","$609"]],"numbersSource":"Source: Kaiser Family Foundation, “How Much Has Medicare Spent on the EpiPen Since 2007?” (2016).","played":["Running market demand P = 100 − 2Q, MC = $20. MR = 100 − 4Q.","MR = MC: 100 − 4Q = 20, so Q = 20 and P = 100 − 40 = $60.","Profit = (60 − 20) × 20 = 800. CS = ½ × 20 × 40 = 400.","Competition would give P = 20, Q = 40. DWL = ½ × (60 − 20) × (40 − 20) = 400."],"graph":"econ380Monopoly","starred":false},{"id":"lerner","n":23,"week":4,"term":"Lerner index / inverse-elasticity rule","simple":"The Lerner index measures market power as the markup over marginal cost as a share of price, and at a monopolist's optimum it equals one over the absolute price elasticity of demand.","formula":"<i>L</i><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>P</i> − MC</span><span><i>P</i></span></span><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span>1</span><span>|ε|</span></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span><i>P</i><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span>MC</span><span>1 − 1/|ε|</span></span>","more":["Derivation: MR = P(1 − 1/|ε|). Set MR = MC and rearrange. The more elastic demand is, the smaller the markup; perfect competition (|ε| → ∞) gives L = 0.","Since MC &gt; 0 requires MR &gt; 0, a monopolist always prices where |ε| &gt; 1, on the elastic part of demand. If a question says a monopolist faces |ε| = 0.5 at its optimum, something is wrong.","Trap: the markup is a share of <b>price</b>, not of cost. |ε| = 4 gives L = 0.25, so P = MC/0.75: a 33% markup on cost, not 25%."],"connections":["mrmc","elas","pricedisc"],"example":"Supermarkets sell staples on thin margins because shoppers can easily switch stores, while a patented drug with no close substitute can carry a very large markup.","numbers":[["Average markup of US listed firms over marginal cost, 1980","21%"],["Same, 2016","61%"]],"numbersSource":"Source: De Loecker, Eeckhout & Unger, “The Rise of Market Power and the Macroeconomic Implications”, Quarterly Journal of Economics (2020); sales-weighted. How to measure markups is debated.","played":["Running monopoly: P = 60, MC = 20, Q = 20.","L = (60 − 20)/60 = 2/3.","Elasticity there: |ε| = (1/2)(60/20) = 1.5, and 1/1.5 = 2/3 ✓.","If instead |ε| = 4 and MC = 30: P = 30/(1 − 1/4) = $40."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Monopoly","args":{"mode":"lerner"}},"starred":false},{"id":"pricedisc","n":24,"week":4,"term":"Price discrimination","simple":"Price discrimination is charging different prices for the same good when the difference isn't due to cost: first degree charges each buyer their reservation price, second degree offers a menu buyers choose from, third degree charges identifiable groups different prices.","formula":"3rd degree: MR<sub>1</sub><span class=\"op\">=</span>MR<sub>2</sub><span class=\"op\">=</span>MC; higher price where |ε| is lower","more":["It needs market power, a way to tell buyers apart (or get them to sort themselves) and no resale.","<b>First degree</b> (perfect): output rises to the competitive level, so there's no DWL, but consumer surplus is zero: the firm takes it all. <b>Second degree</b>: quantity discounts and versions (economy vs business class). <b>Third degree</b>: student, senior and country prices; set MR = MC in each market, and the less elastic group pays more.","Trap: third-degree discrimination can raise or lower total surplus compared with one price. It raises it when it opens a market that a single price wouldn't serve at all."],"connections":["mrmc","lerner","elas","csps"],"example":"In 2014 Gilead's hepatitis C cure Sovaldi had a US list price of about $84,000 per course but was offered for around $900 in India and Egypt, with licensing deals meant to stop resale back to richer markets.","numbers":[["Sovaldi 12-week course, US list price (2014)","$84,000"],["Same, low-income-country price (India, Egypt; 2014)","≈ $900"]],"numbersSource":"Sources: Fierce Pharma and BioCentury reporting on Gilead's tiered pricing (2014).","played":["MC = $20 in two markets. Adults: P = 100 − 2Q. Students: P = 60 − 2Q.","Adults: MR = 100 − 4Q = 20, so Q = 20 and P = $60 (|ε| = (1/2)(60/20) = 1.5).","Students: MR = 60 − 4Q = 20, so Q = 10 and P = $40 (|ε| = (1/2)(40/10) = 2). More elastic, lower price.","Perfect discrimination among adults instead: sell Q = 40, down to P = 20. Profit = ½ × 40 × 80 = 1,600; CS = 0; DWL = 0."],"graph":"econ380PriceDisc","starred":false},{"id":"nash","n":25,"week":5,"term":"Nash equilibrium","simple":"A Nash equilibrium is a set of strategies, one for each player, in which each is a best response to the others, so no player can gain by changing strategy alone.","formula":"<i>u<sub>i</sub></i>(<i>s<sub>i</sub></i>*, <i>s</i><sub>−<i>i</i></sub>*)<span class=\"op\">≥</span><i>u<sub>i</sub></i>(<i>s<sub>i</sub></i>, <i>s</i><sub>−<i>i</i></sub>*) for every <i>s<sub>i</sub></i> and every player <i>i</i>","more":["Finding pure-strategy equilibria in a matrix: in each column, mark the row player's best payoff; in each row, mark the column player's best payoff. Any cell with both marked is a Nash equilibrium.","A game can have several (coordination games), one, or none in pure strategies (matching pennies). Once mixed strategies are allowed, every finite game has at least one (Nash, 1950).","Traps: Nash equilibrium is about <b>one player deviating alone</b>. It need not be efficient (prisoner's dilemma), and it doesn't say how players get there."],"connections":["dominant","pd","cournot","bertrand"],"example":"The Blu-ray vs HD DVD format war was a coordination game: studios and player makers did best on the same format. Both “everyone Blu-ray” and “everyone HD DVD” were equilibria; Toshiba abandoned HD DVD in 2008 and the market settled on Blu-ray.","played":["Coordination game: both pick X → (2, 2); both pick Y → (1, 1); a mismatch → (0, 0).","If the other player picks X, X pays 2 against 0: best response X. If they pick Y, Y pays 1 against 0: best response Y.","Two pure Nash equilibria, (X, X) and (Y, Y). Nobody has a dominant strategy, and (Y, Y) is an equilibrium even though both prefer (X, X)."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Matrix","args":{"game":"coord"}},"starred":false},{"id":"dominant","n":26,"week":5,"term":"Dominant strategy","simple":"A strictly dominant strategy gives a player a higher payoff than any alternative, whatever the other players do.","formula":"<i>u<sub>i</sub></i>(<i>s<sub>i</sub></i>*, <i>s</i><sub>−<i>i</i></sub>)<span class=\"op\">&gt;</span><i>u<sub>i</sub></i>(<i>s<sub>i</sub></i>, <i>s</i><sub>−<i>i</i></sub>) for every <i>s</i><sub>−<i>i</i></sub>","more":["If every player has a dominant strategy, playing them is the unique Nash equilibrium (a dominant-strategy equilibrium). No one needs to predict what others will do.","Even without one, you can <b>iteratively eliminate</b> strictly dominated strategies: a rational player never plays one, so cross it out and look again.","Trap: “dominant” compares your payoffs across <i>your own</i> strategies, holding the other player's choice fixed, not your payoff against theirs. A strategy that wins against one choice and loses against another isn't dominant."],"connections":["nash","pd","bertrand"],"example":"In a sealed-bid second-price (Vickrey) auction, bidding your true value is a weakly dominant strategy: bidding more risks paying more than the item is worth to you, and bidding less only risks losing a profitable win. eBay's proxy bidding works in a similar way.","played":["Pricing game, payoffs (A, B) in $m: both High (10, 10); A Low, B High (12, 2); A High, B Low (2, 12); both Low (5, 5).","If B plays High, A gets 12 from Low against 10 from High. If B plays Low, A gets 5 from Low against 2 from High.","Low is better either way: dominant for A, and by symmetry for B. The equilibrium is (Low, Low) = (5, 5)."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Matrix","args":{"game":"pd"}},"starred":false},{"id":"pd","n":27,"week":5,"term":"Prisoner’s dilemma","simple":"A prisoner's dilemma is a game in which each player has a dominant strategy to defect, yet both would be better off if both cooperated.","formula":"<i>T</i><span class=\"op\">&gt;</span><i>R</i><span class=\"op\">&gt;</span><i>P</i><span class=\"op\">&gt;</span><i>S</i><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span>(temptation, reward, punishment, sucker)","more":["The equilibrium (defect, defect) is Pareto-dominated by (cooperate, cooperate): each player acting rationally on their own doesn't give a good result for the group.","Ways out: <b>repeated</b> play with no known last round lets strategies like tit-for-tat or grim trigger sustain cooperation if players value the future enough; contracts, regulation or reputation can also change the payoffs. With a known final round, backward induction unravels cooperation.","Applications: cartels and price wars, arms races, overfishing and other commons problems, advertising wars."],"connections":["dominant","nash","cournot","pareto"],"example":"OPEC members agree on output quotas to keep oil prices high, but each member gains by quietly pumping more than its quota, and cheating has repeatedly undermined the group's agreements.","played":["Pricing game: (High, High) = (10, 10), (Low, Low) = (5, 5); temptation 12, sucker 2.","Low is dominant for each, so the equilibrium is (Low, Low): $5m each.","Both prefer (High, High): $10m each. T = 12 &gt; R = 10 &gt; P = 5 &gt; S = 2 ✓.","Repeated forever with grim trigger, cooperation holds if 10/(1 − δ) ≥ 12 + 5δ/(1 − δ), that is δ ≥ 2/7 ≈ 0.29."],"graph":{"key":"econ380Matrix","args":{"game":"pd","show":"coop"}},"starred":false},{"id":"cournot","n":28,"week":5,"term":"Cournot duopoly","simple":"In Cournot competition firms choose quantities at the same time, each best-responding to the other's output; total output ends up above the monopoly level but below the competitive level.","formula":"<i>q</i><sub>1</sub><span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>a</i> − <i>c</i> − <i>bq</i><sub>2</sub></span><span>2<i>b</i></span></span><span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span><i>q<sub>i</sub></i>*<span class=\"op\">=</span><span class=\"fr\"><span><i>a</i> − <i>c</i></span><span>3<i>b</i></span></span>","more":["Each firm takes its rival's output as given and acts as a monopolist on the <b>residual demand</b> P = (a − bq<sub>2</sub>) − bq<sub>1</sub>. Reaction functions slope down: the more your rival makes, the less you make.","With n identical firms each makes (a − c)/((n + 1)b), so total output n(a − c)/((n + 1)b) approaches the competitive level as n grows, and price falls toward MC.","Trap: the Cournot outcome is not the collusive one. Together the firms would earn more by splitting monopoly output, but from there each wants to expand: a prisoner's dilemma in quantities."],"connections":["bertrand","nash","pd","mrmc"],"example":"Oil producers decide how much to pump and let the world price clear the market, which is closer to Cournot than to price-setting. Airlines choosing route capacity and memory-chip makers choosing fab capacity are similar.","played":["P = 120 − Q, two firms, MC = 0. Firm 1's MR on residual demand: 120 − q<sub>2</sub> − 2q<sub>1</sub> = 0, so q<sub>1</sub> = (120 − q<sub>2</sub>)/2.","Symmetric: q = (120 − q)/2, so q = 40 each. Q = 80, P = 40, profit 1,600 each.","Monopoly: Q = 60, P = 60, profit 3,600 (1,800 each if split). Competition: Q = 120, P = 0.","Why collusion breaks down: if firm 2 makes 30, firm 1's best response is 45, earning 45 × 45 = 2,025 &gt; 1,800."],"graph":"econ380Cournot","starred":false},{"id":"bertrand","n":29,"week":5,"term":"Bertrand duopoly","simple":"In Bertrand competition firms set prices at the same time; with identical products and equal constant marginal cost, the only equilibrium is price equal to marginal cost, so two firms are enough for the competitive outcome.","formula":"<i>P</i><sub>1</sub><span class=\"op\">=</span><i>P</i><sub>2</sub><span class=\"op\">=</span>MC<span style=\"display:inline-block;width:16px\"></span>profit<span class=\"op\">=</span>0","more":["Logic: if your rival charges above MC, you take the whole market by undercutting by a cent. The undercutting stops only at P = MC, where cutting further means losses.","This is the <b>Bertrand paradox</b>. It goes away with differentiated products, capacity limits, different costs (the low-cost firm prices just below the rival's MC) or repeated interaction.","Cournot vs Bertrand: same demand, very different predictions. When firms fix capacity first and then set prices, the outcome looks like Cournot (Kreps–Scheinkman)."],"connections":["cournot","nash","dominant","lrcomp"],"example":"Price-comparison sites push identical products, such as the same TV model sold by many online retailers, toward Bertrand: prices bunch close to cost. Retailers fight back by differentiating with warranties, bundles and delivery.","played":["Same demand P = 120 − Q, both MC = 0 (think software downloads).","If the rival charges $40 (the Cournot price), charge $39.99 and take all of Q ≈ 80: profit ≈ 3,200 instead of 1,600 shared.","The rival undercuts back, and so on until P = MC = 0, Q = 120, profit 0. Compare monopoly P = 60 and Cournot P = 40."],"graph":"econ380Outcomes","starred":false}]};
const GRAPH_SRC = "// ECON 381 card graphs.\n//\n// Each entry returns { svg, cap } for one card's \"Graph\" section. Drawn as\n// inline SVG from code rather than shipped as images, so they take the\n// card's week colour (via CSS classes in the page), stay sharp at any size\n// and keep the whole deck in one pasteable file.\n//\n// Plain ES5-ish code on purpose: this file is pasted into the review page's\n// <script> as-is, so it must not use backticks or \"${\".\n(function () {\n  var W = 320, H = 210;\n  var X0 = 42, Y0 = 178, X1 = 304, Y1 = 16;   // plot box: origin bottom-left\n\n  function svg(inner, h) {\n    return '<svg viewBox=\"0 0 ' + W + ' ' + (h || H) + '\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">' +\n      '<defs><marker id=\"ah\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\">' +\n      '<path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" fill=\"#2B2118\" opacity=\".7\"/></marker></defs>' + inner + '</svg>';\n  }\n  function t(x, y, s, cls, anchor) {\n    return '<text x=\"' + r(x) + '\" y=\"' + r(y) + '\"' + (cls ? ' class=\"' + cls + '\"' : '') +\n      (anchor ? ' text-anchor=\"' + anchor + '\"' : '') + '>' + s + '</text>';\n  }\n  function ln(x1, y1, x2, y2, cls, arrow) {\n    return '<line x1=\"' + r(x1) + '\" y1=\"' + r(y1) + '\" x2=\"' + r(x2) + '\" y2=\"' + r(y2) + '\" class=\"' + (cls || 'c4') + '\"' +\n      (arrow ? ' marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"' : '') + '/>';\n  }\n  function dot(x, y, cls) { return '<circle cx=\"' + r(x) + '\" cy=\"' + r(y) + '\" r=\"3.6\" class=\"' + (cls || 'dot') + '\"/>'; }\n  function rect(x, y, w, h, cls, rx) {\n    return '<rect x=\"' + r(x) + '\" y=\"' + r(y) + '\" width=\"' + r(w) + '\" height=\"' + r(h) + '\" rx=\"' + (rx == null ? 3 : rx) + '\" class=\"' + cls + '\"/>';\n  }\n  function r(v) { return Math.round(v * 10) / 10; }\n  function pathD(pts) {\n    return pts.map(function (p, k) { return (k ? 'L' : 'M') + r(p[0]) + ' ' + r(p[1]); }).join('');\n  }\n  function curve(pts, cls, arrow) {\n    return '<path d=\"' + pathD(pts) + '\" class=\"' + cls + '\"' + (arrow ? ' marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"' : '') + '/>';\n  }\n  function area(pts, cls) { return '<path d=\"' + pathD(pts) + 'Z\" class=\"' + cls + '\"/>'; }\n  /** Samples f over [a,b] in data units and maps it into the plot box. */\n  function plot(f, a, b, sx, sy, n) {\n    var pts = [];\n    n = n || 60;\n    for (var k = 0; k <= n; k++) {\n      var x = a + (b - a) * k / n;\n      pts.push([sx(x), sy(f(x))]);\n    }\n    return pts;\n  }\n  function scale(d0, d1, p0, p1) { return function (v) { return p0 + (v - d0) * (p1 - p0) / (d1 - d0); }; }\n  function axes(xl, yl) {\n    return ln(X0, Y1 - 4, X0, Y0, 'ax') + ln(X0, Y0, X1 + 4, Y0, 'ax') +\n      t(X1 + 2, Y0 + 15, xl, 'it', 'end') + t(X0 + 6, Y1 + 2, yl, 'it', 'start');\n  }\n  function vguide(x, y, label, cls) {\n    return ln(x, y, x, Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + (label ? t(x, Y0 + 14, label, cls || 'it', 'middle') : '');\n  }\n  function hguide(x, y, label, cls) {\n    return ln(X0, y, x, y, 'c4 dash faint') + (label ? t(X0 - 5, y + 4, label, cls || 'it', 'end') : '');\n  }\n\n  var G = {};\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ week 1\n\n  G.businessCycle = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var trend = function (x) { return 2 + 0.62 * x; };\n    var act = function (x) { return trend(x) + 1.1 * Math.sin(x * 1.25) - (x > 5.6 && x < 7.6 ? 0.9 * Math.sin((x - 5.6) * Math.PI / 2) : 0); };\n    var o = axes('time', 'real GDP');\n    o += rect(sx(5.55), Y1, sx(7.1) - sx(5.55), Y0 - Y1, 'fill2', 0);\n    o += t((sx(5.55) + sx(7.1)) / 2, Y1 + 12, 'recession', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += curve(plot(trend, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c4 dash');\n    o += curve(plot(act, 0, 10, sx, sy, 120), 'c1');\n    o += t(sx(9.9), sy(trend(9.9)) + 16, 'trend', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(1.9), sy(act(1.3)) - 8, 'actual', 'k1', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Output falls below its trend; the shaded stretch is the recession. Rule of thumb: two quarters of falling real GDP.' };\n  };\n\n  G.circularFlow = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += rect(18, 78, 92, 50, 'box1', 12) + t(64, 108, 'Households', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += rect(210, 78, 92, 50, 'box2', 12) + t(256, 108, 'Firms', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[64, 74], [64, 30], [256, 30], [256, 72]], 'c1', true);\n    o += t(160, 24, 'Spending ($) = expenditure', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[256, 132], [256, 178], [64, 178], [64, 134]], 'c2', true);\n    o += t(160, 194, 'Wages, rent, profit ($) = income', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[240, 72], [240, 48], [80, 48], [80, 72]], 'c4 dash', true);\n    o += t(160, 62, 'goods & services', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[80, 134], [80, 158], [240, 158], [240, 134]], 'c4 dash', true);\n    o += t(160, 152, 'labor & capital', 'lb', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 205), cap: 'The same dollars measured three ways: what is spent, what is earned, what is produced. All three equal GDP.' };\n  };\n\n  G.gdpShares = function (a) {\n    var rows = [['C', 'Consumption', 68], ['I', 'Investment', 18], ['G', 'Government', 17], ['NX', 'Net exports', -3]];\n    var sx = scale(-10, 75, 92, 300);\n    var o = ln(sx(0), 14, sx(0), 186, 'c4');\n    rows.forEach(function (row, k) {\n      var y = 22 + k * 42;\n      var on = !a.hi || a.hi === row[0];\n      var x0 = Math.min(sx(0), sx(row[2])), w = Math.abs(sx(row[2]) - sx(0));\n      o += rect(x0, y, w, 26, on ? 'solid1' : 'solid4', 5);\n      o += t(84, y + 12, row[0], 'b', 'end') + t(84, y + 24, row[1], 'lb', 'end');\n      // A negative bar's label goes on the positive side of the zero line, so\n      // it never runs into the row's name.\n      o += t(row[2] < 0 ? sx(0) + 5 : x0 + w + 4, y + 17, (row[2] > 0 ? '' : '−') + Math.abs(row[2]) + '%', on ? 'k1' : 'lb', 'start');\n    });\n    return { svg: svg(o, 190), cap: 'Approximate shares of US GDP, 2025 (BEA). Net exports are negative: the US imports more than it exports.' };\n  };\n\n  G.stockFlow = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += curve([[96, 40], [96, 22], [150, 22]], 'c4');\n    o += rect(140, 16, 22, 12, 'box', 3);\n    o += curve([[151, 28], [151, 70]], 'c1', true);\n    o += t(160, 52, 'inflow per hour = FLOW', 'k1');\n    o += '<path d=\"M70 70 L84 176 Q86 184 96 184 L224 184 Q234 184 236 176 L250 70\" class=\"c4\"/>';\n    o += area([[77, 118], [84, 176], [90, 182], [230, 182], [236, 176], [243, 118]], 'fill1');\n    o += ln(77, 118, 243, 118, 'c1');\n    o += t(160, 156, 'water in the tub = STOCK', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += t(160, 170, '(measured at a moment)', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[236, 176], [266, 196]], 'c2', true);\n    o += t(268, 192, 'outflow', 'k2');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 205), cap: 'Investment (a flow) fills the capital stock; depreciation drains it. GDP is a flow, wealth is a stock.' };\n  };\n\n  G.valueAdded = function () {\n    var steps = [['Farmer', 'wheat', 1, 1], ['Miller', 'flour', 3, 2], ['Baker', 'bread', 5, 2]];\n    var o = '';\n    var sy = scale(0, 5, 170, 30);\n    steps.forEach(function (s, k) {\n      var x = 30 + k * 98;\n      o += rect(x, sy(s[2]), 70, sy(0) - sy(s[2]), 'solid4', 5);\n      o += rect(x, sy(s[2]), 70, sy(0) - sy(s[3]), 'solid1', 5);\n      o += t(x + 35, sy(s[2]) - 6, '$' + s[2] + ' ' + s[1], 'b', 'middle');\n      o += t(x + 35, 186, s[0], 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += t(x + 35, sy(s[2]) + 16, '+' + s[3], 'w', 'middle');\n    });\n    o += t(300, 22, 'GDP = $5', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 196), cap: 'Blue = value added at each stage ($1 + $2 + $2 = $5), which equals the final sale. Adding the full sales ($9) would double count.' };\n  };\n\n  G.indexBias = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(100, 132, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('years from base', 'index');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 100 + 2.9 * x; }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c2');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 100 + 2.6 * x; }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c4 dash');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 100 + 2.3 * x; }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += t(sx(10), sy(129) - 6, 'Laspeyres (CPI)', 'k2', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(10), sy(126) - 6, 'true cost of living', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(10), sy(123) + 14, 'Paasche (deflator)', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += t(X0 - 6, sy(100) + 4, '100', 'lb', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Stylised. The fixed basket ignores switching to cheaper goods, so it runs high; the current basket gives too little weight to what got dearer, so it runs low.' };\n  };\n\n  G.cpiHistory = function () {\n    var d = [[2019, 1.8], [2020, 1.2], [2021, 4.7], [2022, 8.0], [2023, 4.1], [2024, 2.9]];\n    var sx = scale(2018.4, 2024.6, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 9, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('', '%');\n    [2, 4, 6, 8].forEach(function (v) { o += ln(X0, sy(v), X1, sy(v), 'grid') + t(X0 - 5, sy(v) + 4, v, 'lb', 'end'); });\n    d.forEach(function (p) {\n      o += rect(sx(p[0]) - 15, sy(p[1]), 30, Y0 - sy(p[1]), p[0] === 2022 ? 'solid2' : 'solid1', 4);\n      o += t(sx(p[0]), sy(p[1]) - 5, p[1].toFixed(1), p[0] === 2022 ? 'k2' : 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += t(sx(p[0]), Y0 + 14, String(p[0]), 'lb', 'middle');\n    });\n    return { svg: svg(o, 200), cap: 'US CPI-U inflation, annual average (BLS). 2022 was the highest since 1981.' };\n  };\n\n  G.laborForceTree = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += rect(95, 8, 130, 34, 'box', 10) + t(160, 24, 'Adult population', 'b', 'middle') + t(160, 36, '16+, not institutionalised', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[160, 42], [160, 54], [82, 54], [82, 66]], 'c4', true);\n    o += curve([[160, 54], [240, 54], [240, 66]], 'c4', true);\n    o += rect(14, 68, 136, 30, 'box1', 10) + t(82, 87, 'Labor force  L', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += rect(170, 68, 140, 30, 'box', 10) + t(240, 87, 'Not in labor force', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[50, 98], [50, 112]], 'c4', true) + curve([[114, 98], [114, 112]], 'c4', true);\n    o += rect(14, 114, 70, 46, 'box3', 10) + t(49, 133, 'Employed', 'k3', 'middle') + t(49, 148, 'E', 'it', 'middle');\n    o += rect(88, 114, 62, 46, 'box2', 10) + t(119, 133, 'Unem-', 'k2', 'middle') + t(119, 146, 'ployed  U', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += rect(170, 114, 140, 46, 'box', 10) + t(240, 131, 'retirees, students,', 'lb', 'middle') + t(240, 145, 'homemakers,', 'lb', 'middle') + t(240, 156, 'discouraged workers', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(82, 182, 'u = U ⁄ (E + U)', 'it', 'middle');\n    o += t(240, 182, 'LFPR = L ⁄ adult pop.', 'it', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 192), cap: 'A discouraged worker who stops searching moves from U to \"not in the labor force\": the unemployment rate falls though nothing improved.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ week 2\n\n  G.production = function (a) {\n    var v = a.x || 'L';\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var f = function (x) { return 3 * Math.pow(x, 0.5); };\n    var o = axes(v, 'Y');\n    o += curve(plot(f, 0, 10, sx, sy, 80), 'c1');\n    [2.2, 7].forEach(function (x0) {\n      var m = 1.5 / Math.sqrt(x0);\n      o += ln(sx(x0 - 1.6), sy(f(x0) - 1.6 * m), sx(x0 + 1.6), sy(f(x0) + 1.6 * m), 'c2');\n      o += dot(sx(x0), sy(f(x0)));\n    });\n    o += t(sx(2.2) + 4, sy(f(2.2)) + 18, 'steep: high MP' + v, 'k2');\n    o += t(sx(7) + 2, sy(f(7)) + 20, 'flatter: low MP' + v, 'k2');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(f(9.8)) - 8, 'Y = F(K, L)', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Holding the other input fixed, each extra unit of ' + v + ' adds less output: diminishing marginal product. The slope is MP' + v + '.' };\n  };\n\n  G.factorMarket = function (a) {\n    var cap = a.kind === 'capital';\n    var v = cap ? 'K' : 'L', price = cap ? 'R/P' : 'W/P', mp = cap ? 'MPK' : 'MPL';\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var d = function (x) { return 9.4 / (0.55 + 0.32 * x); };\n    var o = axes(v, price);\n    o += curve(plot(d, 1.4, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += ln(sx(5.5), Y1, sx(5.5), Y0, 'c2');\n    o += t(sx(5.5) + 4, Y1 + 10, cap ? 'K̅ supply' : 'L̅ supply', 'k2');\n    o += hguide(sx(5.5), sy(d(5.5)), price + '*', 'k1');\n    o += dot(sx(5.5), sy(d(5.5)));\n    o += t(sx(9.9), sy(d(9.9)) - 8, mp + ' = demand', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Firms hire ' + v + ' until ' + mp + ' = ' + price + '. With the supply fixed, the ' + mp + ' at that quantity sets the real ' + (cap ? 'rental price' : 'wage') + '.' };\n  };\n\n  G.incomeSplit = function (a) {\n    var alpha = a.alpha || 1 / 3;\n    var cx = 108, cy = 98, R = 76, rr = 44;\n    function arc(a0, a1, cls) {\n      var p = function (ang, rad) { return [cx + rad * Math.sin(ang), cy - rad * Math.cos(ang)]; };\n      var A = p(a0, R), B = p(a1, R), C = p(a1, rr), D = p(a0, rr);\n      var big = a1 - a0 > Math.PI ? 1 : 0;\n      return '<path class=\"' + cls + '\" d=\"M' + r(A[0]) + ' ' + r(A[1]) + 'A' + R + ' ' + R + ' 0 ' + big + ' 1 ' + r(B[0]) + ' ' + r(B[1]) +\n        'L' + r(C[0]) + ' ' + r(C[1]) + 'A' + rr + ' ' + rr + ' 0 ' + big + ' 0 ' + r(D[0]) + ' ' + r(D[1]) + 'Z\"/>';\n    }\n    var cut = 2 * Math.PI * (1 - alpha);\n    var o = arc(0, cut, 'solid1') + arc(cut, 2 * Math.PI - 0.0001, 'solid2');\n    o += t(cx, cy + 4, 'Y', 'it b', 'middle');\n    o += rect(208, 64, 14, 14, 'solid1', 3) + t(228, 75, 'Labor  1 − α', 'k1');\n    o += t(228, 89, '≈ ' + Math.round((1 - alpha) * 100) + '% = WL/PY', 'lb');\n    o += rect(208, 108, 14, 14, 'solid2', 3) + t(228, 119, 'Capital  α', 'k2');\n    o += t(228, 133, '≈ ' + Math.round(alpha * 100) + '% = RK/PY', 'lb');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 186), cap: 'With Cobb-Douglas and competitive markets the pie is split in fixed shares, and the two slices use up all of output.' };\n  };\n\n  G.laborShareShift = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(50, 66, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('time', 'labor share %');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 63.5 - 0.15 * Math.sin(x * 2.2) - (x > 5 ? 0.9 * (x - 5) : 0); }, 0, 10, sx, sy, 80), 'c1');\n    o += hguide(sx(5), sy(63.4), '', '');\n    o += t(sx(2.3), sy(64.6), 'roughly flat: Kaldor fact', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(9.6), sy(58.6) + 6, 'falls since ~2000', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(9.6), sy(55) + 4, 'automation, offshoring,', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(9.6), sy(55) + 16, 'rising markups', 'lb', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Stylised. Cobb-Douglas says the share should be constant; the data show a decline, which is why the course asks you for explanations.' };\n  };\n\n  G.consumption = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('Y − T', 'C');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return x; }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 2 + 0.6 * x; }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += ln(sx(4), sy(4.4), sx(6.5), sy(4.4), 'c2') + ln(sx(6.5), sy(4.4), sx(6.5), sy(5.9), 'c2');\n    o += t(sx(5.25), sy(4.4) + 14, '+$1', 'k2', 'middle') + t(sx(6.5) + 4, sy(5.15) + 4, '+$MPC', 'k2');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(7.9) - 8, 'C = C(Y − T)', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(9.8) - 6, '45°', 'lb', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'The slope of the consumption function is the MPC, between 0 and 1: of each extra dollar of disposable income, part is spent and the rest is saved.' };\n  };\n\n  G.investment = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('I', 'r');\n    var f = function (x) { return 9.5 - 0.85 * x; };\n    o += curve(plot(f, 0.5, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += dot(sx(3), sy(f(3))) + dot(sx(6.5), sy(f(6.5)));\n    o += hguide(sx(3), sy(f(3)), 'r₁') + vguide(sx(3), sy(f(3)), 'I₁');\n    o += hguide(sx(6.5), sy(f(6.5)), 'r₂') + vguide(sx(6.5), sy(f(6.5)), 'I₂');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(f(9.8)) - 8, 'I(r)', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'A higher real interest rate raises the cost of borrowing (or the return given up), so fewer projects pay off and investment falls.' };\n  };\n\n  G.loanableFunds = function (a) {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var f = function (x) { return 9.5 - 0.85 * x; };\n    var o = axes('S, I', 'r');\n    o += curve(plot(f, 0.5, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(f(9.8)) - 8, 'I(r)', 'k1', 'end');\n    var s1 = 5.5;\n    if (!a.shift) {\n      o += ln(sx(s1), Y1, sx(s1), Y0, 'c2') + t(sx(s1) + 4, Y1 + 10, 'S = Y̅ − C − G', 'k2');\n      o += dot(sx(s1), sy(f(s1))) + hguide(sx(s1), sy(f(s1)), 'r*', 'k1');\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Saving does not depend on r here, so the supply of loanable funds is vertical. The real interest rate moves until investment demand equals it.' };\n    }\n    var s2 = a.shift === 'left' ? 3.6 : 7.2;\n    o += ln(sx(s1), Y1, sx(s1), Y0, 'c2 faint') + t(sx(s1) + 3, Y1 + 10, 'S₁', 'k2');\n    o += ln(sx(s2), Y1, sx(s2), Y0, 'c2') + t(sx(s2) + 3, Y1 + 10, 'S₂', 'k2');\n    o += curve([[sx(s1) - 2, sy(8.9)], [sx(s2) + (s2 < s1 ? 6 : -6), sy(8.9)]], 'c4', true);\n    o += dot(sx(s1), sy(f(s1)), 'solid4') + dot(sx(s2), sy(f(s2)));\n    o += hguide(sx(s1), sy(f(s1)), 'r₁') + hguide(sx(s2), sy(f(s2)), 'r₂', 'k1');\n    var cap = a.shift === 'left'\n      ? (a.cap || 'G rises (or T falls): public saving falls, S shifts left, r rises and investment falls by the same amount. That is crowding out.')\n      : (a.cap || 'T rises: public saving rises by ΔT, private saving falls by MPC·ΔT, so S shifts right by (1 − MPC)ΔT; r falls and investment rises.');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: cap };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ week 3\n\n  G.hyperinflation = function () {\n    var d = [['1914', 4.2], ['Jan 20', 65], ['Jul 22', 493], ['Jan 23', 17972], ['Jul 23', 353412], ['Sep 23', 9.89e7], ['Nov 23', 4.2e12]];\n    var sx = scale(0, d.length - 1, X0 + 16, X1 - 6), sy = scale(0, 13, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('', 'marks per $ (log)');\n    [0, 3, 6, 9, 12].forEach(function (p) {\n      o += ln(X0, sy(p), X1, sy(p), 'grid') + t(X0 - 5, sy(p) + 4, p === 0 ? '1' : '10' + '<tspan dy=\"-5\" font-size=\"8\">' + p + '</tspan>', 'lb', 'end');\n    });\n    var pts = d.map(function (p, k) { return [sx(k), sy(Math.log(p[1]) / Math.LN10)]; });\n    o += curve(pts, 'c2');\n    pts.forEach(function (p, k) { o += dot(p[0], p[1], 'solid2') + t(p[0], Y0 + 14, d[k][0], 'lb', 'middle'); });\n    o += t(pts[6][0] - 6, pts[6][1] + 4, '4.2 trillion', 'k2', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 200), cap: 'German paper marks per US dollar. From 4.2 in 1914 to 4.2 trillion in November 1923: prices rose by roughly a trillion times.' };\n  };\n\n  G.moneyAggregates = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += t(16, 30, 'Monetary base', 'b') + t(16, 42, '(the Fed controls)', 'lb');\n    o += rect(120, 18, 70, 30, 'solid1', 6) + t(155, 37, 'currency', 'w', 'middle');\n    o += rect(192, 18, 110, 30, 'box1', 6) + t(247, 37, 'bank reserves', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += t(16, 92, 'M1', 'b') + t(16, 104, '(spendable money)', 'lb');\n    o += rect(120, 80, 70, 30, 'solid1', 6) + t(155, 99, 'currency', 'w', 'middle');\n    o += rect(192, 80, 54, 30, 'box3', 6) + t(219, 93, 'check-', 'k3', 'middle') + t(219, 104, 'able', 'k3', 'middle');\n    o += rect(248, 80, 54, 30, 'box2', 6) + t(275, 93, 'savings', 'k2', 'middle') + t(275, 104, 'since ’20', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(16, 152, 'M2', 'b') + t(16, 164, '= M1 + small time', 'lb') + t(16, 175, 'deposits + money funds', 'lb');\n    o += rect(120, 142, 182, 30, 'box', 6) + t(211, 161, 'broader still', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[155, 50], [155, 76]], 'c4 dash', true);\n    return { svg: svg(o, 190), cap: 'Currency is in both. Reserves are in the base but not in M1; deposits are in M1 but not in the base. So the base is NOT \"all currency and deposits\".' };\n  };\n\n  G.quantityTheory = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 30, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 30, Y0, Y1);\n    var pts = [[2, 1.5], [4, 2.8], [5, 4.2], [7, 5.1], [8, 7.5], [10, 8.2], [12, 10.5], [14, 13.6], [17, 15], [19, 18.5], [22, 19.6], [25, 24.1], [28, 26.5], [3, 3.6], [6, 3.4], [9, 6.2], [15, 12.4]];\n    var o = axes('money growth %ΔM', 'π %');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return x - 2.5; }, 2.5, 30, sx, sy), 'c4 dash');\n    pts.forEach(function (p) { o += dot(sx(p[0]), sy(p[1]), 'solid1'); });\n    o += t(sx(29), sy(26.5) - 12, 'π = %ΔM − %ΔY', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Stylised cross-country picture: countries with faster money growth have higher inflation, about one for one, shifted down by real growth.' };\n  };\n\n  G.moneyDemand = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var f = function (x) { return 9.6 / (0.5 + 0.3 * x) - 0.7; };\n    var o = axes('M/P', 'i');\n    o += curve(plot(f, 1.5, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += ln(sx(5), Y1, sx(5), Y0, 'c2') + t(sx(5) + 4, Y1 + 10, '(M/P)ˢ', 'k2');\n    o += dot(sx(5), sy(f(5))) + hguide(sx(5), sy(f(5)), 'i*', 'k1');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(f(9.8)) - 8, 'L(i, Y)', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Money pays no interest, so a higher nominal rate means you hold less of it: you spend it faster, and velocity rises.' };\n  };\n\n  G.fisher = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    var sy = scale(0, 8, 168, 20);\n    function bar(x, rr, pi, label) {\n      var s = rect(x, sy(rr), 64, sy(0) - sy(rr), 'solid3', 4);\n      s += rect(x, sy(rr + pi), 64, sy(rr) - sy(rr + pi), 'solid2', 4);\n      s += t(x + 32, sy(rr / 2) + 4, 'r = ' + rr, 'w', 'middle');\n      s += t(x + 32, sy(rr + pi / 2) + 4, 'π = ' + pi, 'w', 'middle');\n      s += t(x + 32, sy(rr + pi) - 6, 'i = ' + (rr + pi) + '%', 'b', 'middle');\n      s += t(x + 32, 184, label, 'lb', 'middle');\n      return s;\n    }\n    o += bar(60, 2, 3, 'before') + bar(196, 2, 5, 'expected π +2');\n    o += curve([[132, 90], [188, 70]], 'c4', true);\n    return { svg: svg(o, 192), cap: 'Fisher effect: when expected inflation rises 2 points, the nominal rate rises 2 points and the real rate (green) stays put.' };\n  };\n\n  G.inflationVsRates = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    var rows = [['Fed funds target', '3.75–4.00%', 'Sep 16, 2026'], ['CPI inflation, 12 mo.', '3.4%', 'Aug 2026'], ['Real policy rate ≈', '+0.5%', 'i − π']];\n    rows.forEach(function (row, k) {\n      var y = 26 + k * 50;\n      o += rect(16, y - 16, 288, 40, k === 2 ? 'box1' : 'box', 10);\n      o += t(30, y + 4, row[0], k === 2 ? 'k1' : 'b');\n      o += t(290, y + 4, row[1], k === 2 ? 'k1' : 'b', 'end');\n      o += t(30, y + 17, row[2], 'lb');\n    });\n    return { svg: svg(o, 176), cap: 'Using ex post inflation. With expected inflation in place of actual, you get the ex ante real rate instead.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ week 4\n\n  G.flows = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += '<circle cx=\"78\" cy=\"96\" r=\"50\" class=\"box3\"/>' + t(78, 92, 'Employed', 'k3', 'middle') + t(78, 108, 'E', 'it', 'middle');\n    o += '<circle cx=\"242\" cy=\"96\" r=\"50\" class=\"box2\"/>' + t(242, 92, 'Unemployed', 'k2', 'middle') + t(242, 108, 'U', 'it', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[118, 66], [160, 40], [204, 66]], 'c2', true);\n    o += t(160, 32, 'job losses  s · E', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[204, 126], [160, 152], [118, 126]], 'c3', true);\n    o += t(160, 170, 'job finding  f · U', 'k3', 'middle');\n    o += t(160, 194, 'steady state: s·E = f·U  ⇒  U/L = s/(s + f)', 'it', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 202), cap: 'Each period a share s of the employed lose jobs and a share f of the unemployed find one. When the two flows match, unemployment stops changing.' };\n  };\n\n  G.unemploymentPath = function () {\n    var s = 0.02, f = 0.28, u = 0.12, pts = [];\n    var sx = scale(0, 14, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 13, Y0, Y1);\n    for (var k = 0; k <= 14; k++) { pts.push([sx(k), sy(u * 100)]); u = u + s * (1 - u) - f * u; }\n    var o = axes('months', 'u %');\n    var ss = s / (s + f) * 100;\n    o += ln(X0, sy(ss), X1, sy(ss), 'c2 dash') + t(X1, sy(ss) - 6, 'u* = 6.67%', 'k2', 'end');\n    o += curve(pts, 'c1');\n    pts.forEach(function (p) { o += dot(p[0], p[1], 'solid1'); });\n    o += t(X0 - 5, sy(12) + 4, '12', 'lb', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Start at 12% with s = 0.02 and f = 0.28: unemployment falls quickly toward s/(s + f) = 6.67% and stays there.' };\n  };\n\n  G.wageFloor = function (a) {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var D = function (x) { return 9.2 - 0.8 * x; }, S = function (x) { return 1 + 0.8 * x; };\n    var o = axes('labor', 'W/P');\n    o += curve(plot(D, 0.5, 10, sx, sy), 'c1') + t(sx(9.9), sy(D(9.9)) - 8, 'demand', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += curve(plot(S, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c3') + t(sx(9.9), sy(S(9.9)) + 14, 'supply', 'k3', 'end');\n    var w = 7.2, ld = (9.2 - w) / 0.8, ls = (w - 1) / 0.8;\n    o += ln(X0, sy(w), X1, sy(w), 'c2') + t(X0 + 4, sy(w) - 6, a.label || 'wage floor', 'k2');\n    o += dot(sx(ld), sy(w)) + dot(sx(ls), sy(w));\n    o += ln(sx(ld), sy(w) + 14, sx(ls), sy(w) + 14, 'c2', true);\n    o += t((sx(ld) + sx(ls)) / 2, sy(w) + 28, 'unemployed', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += dot(sx(5.125), sy(D(5.125)), 'solid4');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'With the real wage held above where supply meets demand, more people want work than firms want to hire. The gap is structural unemployment.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ week 5\n\n  G.solow = function (a) {\n    var mode = a.mode || 'basic';\n    var alpha = 0.5, s = 0.3, d = 0.1, n = 0.04;\n    var f = function (k) { return Math.pow(k, alpha); };\n    // The golden-rule picture needs room for k_gold = 25 and f(k) itself.\n    var kmax = mode === 'golden' ? 40 : 14;\n    var sx = scale(0, kmax, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, mode === 'golden' ? 7 : 1.25, Y0, Y1);\n    // Straight lines stop where they reach the top of the plot.\n    var kTop = mode === 'golden' ? kmax * 0.95 : Math.min(kmax * 0.95, 1.2 / d);\n    var kss = function (sv, dd) { return Math.pow(sv / dd, 1 / (1 - alpha)); };\n    var o = axes('k', '');\n    var cap;\n    if (mode === 'basic' || mode === 'diagram') {\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return s * f(k); }, 0, kmax, sx, sy, 90), 'c1') + t(sx(kmax), sy(s * f(kmax)) + 14, 's f(k)', 'k1', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return d * k; }, 0, kTop, sx, sy), 'c2') + t(sx(kTop) - 6, sy(d * kTop) + 4, 'δk', 'k2', 'end');\n      var k1 = kss(s, d);\n      o += dot(sx(k1), sy(d * k1)) + vguide(sx(k1), sy(d * k1), 'k*', 'k1');\n      o += curve([[sx(2.4), sy(0.36)], [sx(5.4), sy(0.36)]], 'c4', true) + t(sx(2.4), sy(0.36) - 6, 'k rises', 'lb');\n      o += curve([[sx(13), sy(0.62)], [sx(10.4), sy(0.62)]], 'c4', true) + t(sx(13), sy(0.62) - 6, 'k falls', 'lb', 'end');\n      cap = mode === 'diagram'\n        ? 'Label everything: axes (k, and investment/depreciation per worker), s f(k), δk (and f(k) above them if you draw it), the crossing k*, and arrows showing k moving toward it from both sides.'\n        : 'Left of k*, investment s f(k) beats depreciation δk and k grows; right of it, depreciation wins. They meet at the steady state.';\n    } else if (mode === 'saving') {\n      var s2 = 0.4;\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return s * f(k); }, 0, kmax, sx, sy, 90), 'c1 faint') + t(sx(kmax), sy(s * f(kmax)) + 14, 's₁ f(k)', 'k1', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return s2 * f(k); }, 0, kmax, sx, sy, 90), 'c1') + t(sx(kmax), sy(s2 * f(kmax)) - 6, 's₂ f(k)', 'k1', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return d * k; }, 0, kTop, sx, sy), 'c2');\n      var a1 = kss(s, d), a2 = kss(s2, d);\n      o += dot(sx(a1), sy(d * a1), 'solid4') + vguide(sx(a1), sy(d * a1), 'k₁*');\n      o += dot(sx(a2), sy(d * a2)) + vguide(sx(a2), sy(d * a2), 'k₂*', 'k1');\n      cap = 'A higher saving rate lifts s f(k): the steady state moves right, so income per worker ends up higher, but growth stops again once k reaches k₂*.';\n    } else if (mode === 'pop') {\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return s * f(k); }, 0, kmax, sx, sy, 90), 'c1') + t(sx(kmax), sy(s * f(kmax)) + 14, 's f(k)', 'k1', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return d * k; }, 0, kTop, sx, sy), 'c2 faint') + t(sx(kTop) - 6, sy(d * kTop) + 4, 'δk', 'k2', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return (d + n) * k; }, 0, 8.6, sx, sy), 'c2') + t(sx(8.6), sy((d + n) * 8.6) - 6, '(δ + n)k', 'k2', 'end');\n      var b1 = kss(s, d), b2 = kss(s, d + n);\n      o += dot(sx(b1), sy(d * b1), 'solid4') + vguide(sx(b1), sy(d * b1), 'k*');\n      o += dot(sx(b2), sy((d + n) * b2)) + vguide(sx(b2), sy((d + n) * b2), 'k*ₙ', 'k2');\n      cap = 'Population growth n is like extra depreciation: new workers need capital too. The break-even line steepens and steady-state k falls.';\n    } else {\n      var kg = Math.pow(alpha / d, 1 / (1 - alpha));\n      o += curve(plot(f, 0, kmax, sx, sy, 90), 'c1') + t(sx(kmax), sy(f(kmax)) - 6, 'f(k)', 'k1', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return d * k; }, 0, kmax * 0.95, sx, sy), 'c2') + t(sx(kmax * 0.95), sy(d * kmax * 0.95) + 14, 'δk', 'k2', 'end');\n      o += ln(sx(kg), sy(f(kg)), sx(kg), sy(d * kg), 'c3', false);\n      o += t(sx(kg) + 5, sy((f(kg) + d * kg) / 2), 'c* biggest', 'k3');\n      o += ln(sx(kg - 3), sy(f(kg) - 3 * d), sx(kg + 3), sy(f(kg) + 3 * d), 'c3 dash');\n      o += vguide(sx(kg), sy(d * kg), 'k_gold', 'k3');\n      cap = 'Consumption per worker is the gap between f(k) and δk. It is widest where the slope of f(k) equals δ, i.e. MPK = δ.';\n    }\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: cap };\n  };\n\n  G.solowShock = function (a) {\n    var v = a.v || 'y';\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('time', v === 'c' ? 'c' : v === 'Y' ? 'Y (total)' : 'y = Y/L');\n    var base = 4.2, t0 = 3;\n    var path = function (x) { return base + 2.6 * Math.exp(-(x - t0) * 0.55); };\n    if (v === 'Y') {\n      var Yp = function (x) { return 4.2 + 0.8 * Math.exp(-(x - t0) * 0.55); };\n      o += curve([[sx(0), sy(6.4)], [sx(t0), sy(6.4)]], 'c1');\n      o += ln(sx(t0), sy(6.4), sx(t0), sy(Yp(t0)), 'c1 dash');\n      o += curve(plot(Yp, t0, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n      o += t(sx(t0), Y0 + 14, 'L falls', 'k2', 'middle');\n      o += t(sx(t0) + 6, sy(5.9), 'Y drops with L,', 'k1');\n      o += t(sx(t0) + 6, sy(5.3), 'then K shrinks too', 'lb');\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Total output Y falls: fewer workers, and over time less capital. Per-worker output only rises for a while.' };\n    }\n    o += curve([[sx(0), sy(base)], [sx(t0), sy(base)]], 'c1');\n    o += ln(sx(t0), sy(base), sx(t0), sy(base + 2.6), 'c1 dash');\n    o += curve(plot(path, t0, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += ln(X0, sy(base), X1, sy(base), 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += t(sx(t0), Y0 + 14, 'L falls', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(base) - 6, 'same steady state', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(t0) + 6, sy(base + 2.5), v === 'c' ? 'c jumps up' : 'k and y jump up', 'k1');\n    o += t(sx(4.6), sy(base + 1.3), 'then falls back', 'lb');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: v === 'c'\n      ? 'With s unchanged, c = (1 − s)y moves with y: it jumps up, then falls back to where it started.'\n      : 'Fewer workers share the same capital, so k = K/L jumps above k*. Depreciation then outruns investment and k and y slide back to the old steady state.' };\n  };\n\n  G.savingPath = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var t0 = 3;\n    var o = axes('time', 'y');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return x < t0 ? 3 : 3 + 3.5 * (1 - Math.exp(-(x - t0) * 0.5)); }, 0, 10, sx, sy, 80), 'c1');\n    o += ln(X0, sy(6.5), X1, sy(6.5), 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += t(sx(t0), Y0 + 14, 's rises', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(6.5) - 6, 'higher level', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(4.6), sy(3.6), 'faster growth', 'lb');\n    o += t(sx(4.6), sy(2.8), 'only for a while', 'lb');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'A permanent rise in the saving rate gives a burst of growth and a permanently higher level of y, not a permanently higher growth rate.' };\n  };\n\n  G.goldenHump = function () {\n    var alpha = 0.33;\n    var sx = scale(0, 1, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 1.05, Y0, Y1);\n    var c = function (s) { return s <= 0 || s >= 1 ? 0 : (1 - s) * Math.pow(s, alpha / (1 - alpha)) / Math.pow(alpha, alpha / (1 - alpha)) / (1 - alpha); };\n    var o = axes('saving rate s', 'c*');\n    o += curve(plot(c, 0.001, 0.999, sx, sy, 120), 'c1');\n    o += dot(sx(alpha), sy(c(alpha))) + vguide(sx(alpha), sy(c(alpha)), 's_gold = α', 'k3');\n    o += t(sx(0.2), sy(0.22), 'below: save more', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(0.73), sy(0.92), 'above: save less', 'lb', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Steady-state consumption peaks when the saving rate equals capital’s share α. Saving 0% or 100% both leave nothing to eat.' };\n  };\n\n  G.convergence = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('time', 'y');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 8.4 - 0.9 * Math.exp(-x * 0.4); }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c4');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 8.4 - 6.5 * Math.exp(-x * 0.4); }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += t(sx(0.2), sy(7.5) - 8, 'rich: near steady state', 'lb');\n    o += t(sx(0.4), sy(1.9) + 16, 'war-damaged: grows fast', 'k1');\n    o += ln(X0, sy(8.4), X1, sy(8.4), 'c4 dash faint');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'An economy far below its steady state has a high return on capital and grows fast; growth slows as it closes the gap (postwar Japan, Germany).' };\n  };\n\n  G.chinaGrowth = function () {\n    var d = [[2010, 10.6], [2012, 7.9], [2014, 7.4], [2016, 6.8], [2018, 6.7], [2019, 6.0], [2020, 2.2], [2021, 8.4], [2022, 3.0], [2023, 5.2], [2024, 5.0], [2025, 5.0]];\n    var sx = scale(-0.6, d.length - 0.4, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 12, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('', '%');\n    [4, 8, 12].forEach(function (v) { o += ln(X0, sy(v), X1, sy(v), 'grid') + t(X0 - 5, sy(v) + 4, v, 'lb', 'end'); });\n    d.forEach(function (p, k) {\n      o += rect(sx(k) - 8, sy(p[1]), 16, Y0 - sy(p[1]), 'solid1', 3);\n      o += t(sx(k), Y0 + 13, \"'\" + String(p[0]).slice(2), 'lb', 'middle');\n    });\n    o += t(sx(0), sy(10.6) - 5, '10.6', 'k1', 'middle') + t(sx(11), sy(5) - 5, '5.0', 'k1', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 196), cap: 'China’s official real GDP growth (NBS). From double digits to about 5%; outside estimates put recent years lower still.' };\n  };\n\n  G.crs = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    function grid(x, n, cls) {\n      var s = '';\n      for (var k = 0; k < n; k++) s += rect(x + (k % 2) * 26, 30 + Math.floor(k / 2) * 26, 22, 22, cls, 4);\n      return s;\n    }\n    o += t(60, 20, 'K, L', 'b', 'middle') + grid(34, 2, 'solid1');\n    o += t(60, 100, 'Y', 'b', 'middle') + rect(40, 108, 40, 22, 'solid2', 4);\n    o += curve([[110, 70], [190, 70]], 'c4', true) + t(150, 62, '× 2', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += t(250, 20, '2K, 2L', 'b', 'middle') + grid(224, 4, 'solid1');\n    o += t(250, 100, '2Y', 'b', 'middle') + rect(206, 108, 40, 22, 'solid2', 4) + rect(252, 108, 40, 22, 'solid2', 4);\n    o += t(160, 160, 'F(zK, zL) = z · F(K, L)', 'it', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 172), cap: 'Double every input, get exactly double the output. Cobb-Douglas has this because the exponents α and 1 − α add to 1.' };\n  };\n\n  G.wageCompression = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('wage percentile', 'wage growth');\n    var pts = [[1, 8.6], [2.5, 7.4], [4, 6.2], [5.5, 5.3], [7, 4.6], [8.5, 4.1], [9.6, 3.8]];\n    o += curve(pts.map(function (p) { return [sx(p[0]), sy(p[1])]; }), 'c1');\n    pts.forEach(function (p) { o += dot(sx(p[0]), sy(p[1]), 'solid1'); });\n    o += t(sx(1), Y0 + 14, '10th', 'lb', 'middle') + t(sx(5.5), Y0 + 14, '50th', 'lb', 'middle') + t(sx(9.6), Y0 + 14, '90th', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(1.4), sy(8.6) - 8, 'fastest at the bottom', 'k1');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Stylised. In the tight post-pandemic labor market, wages rose fastest for the lowest paid, narrowing the gap with the middle and top.' };\n  };\n\n  window.GRAPHS = G;\n  // The drawing helpers, for the other classes' graph files (tools/graphs/<id>.js),\n  // which add their own entries to window.GRAPHS in the same style.\n  window.GRAPHKIT = { svg: svg, t: t, ln: ln, dot: dot, rect: rect, r: r, pathD: pathD, curve: curve, area: area,\n    plot: plot, scale: scale, axes: axes, vguide: vguide, hguide: hguide, W: W, H: H, X0: X0, Y0: Y0, X1: X1, Y1: Y1 };\n})();\n\n// ECON 380 · Intermediate Micro Theory: the class's card and practice graphs,\n// added to the shared library (tools/graphs.js) with its drawing helpers.\n//\n// The numbers match the cards' running examples:\n//   consumer   U = x^0.5 y^0.5, I = 100, px = 2, py = 5 → (25, 10); px → 8\n//   production Q = K^0.5 L^0.5, w = 4, r = 1, Q = 20 → L = 10, K = 40\n//   costs      TC = 32 + q³ − 6q² + 20q: min AVC 11 at q = 3, min ATC 20 at q = 4\n//   market     P = 100 − 2Q, P = 10 + Q → Q = 30, P = 40; $6 tax; $34 ceiling\n//   monopoly   P = 100 − 2Q, MC = 20 → Q = 20, P = 60\n//   games      pricing PD; coordination game; Cournot P = 120 − Q, MC = 0\n//\n// Plain ES5 on purpose: pasted into the page as-is (no backticks or \"${\").\n(function () {\n  var K = window.GRAPHKIT, G = window.GRAPHS;\n  var svg = K.svg, t = K.t, ln = K.ln, dot = K.dot, rect = K.rect, curve = K.curve, area = K.area,\n    plot = K.plot, scale = K.scale, axes = K.axes, vguide = K.vguide, hguide = K.hguide;\n  var X0 = K.X0, Y0 = K.Y0, X1 = K.X1, Y1 = K.Y1;\n\n  /** base with a subscript, then (optionally) more text back on the baseline. */\n  function sb(base, s, rest) {\n    return base + '<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"8.5\">' + s + '</tspan>' + (rest ? '<tspan dy=\"-3\">' + rest + '</tspan>' : '');\n  }\n  /** Axes for a panel with its own box (for side-by-side diagrams). */\n  function panelAxes(x0, y0, x1, y1, xl, yl) {\n    return ln(x0, y1 - 4, x0, y0, 'ax') + ln(x0, y0, x1 + 4, y0, 'ax') +\n      t(x1 + 2, y0 + 15, xl, 'it', 'end') + t(x0 + 6, y1 + 2, yl, 'it', 'start');\n  }\n  /** Two short slashes on the vertical axis: it doesn't start at zero. */\n  function brk(x, y) {\n    return ln(x - 5, y + 3, x + 5, y - 1, 'c4') + ln(x - 5, y + 7, x + 5, y + 3, 'c4');\n  }\n\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------- consumer\n\n\n  /** Labels a curve just past its right end, on the curve's own height. */\n  function endLabel(x, y, s, cls) { return t(x + 4, y + 4, s, cls, 'start'); }\n  /** A two-headed arrow between two points. */\n  function both(x1, y1, x2, y2) { return curve([[x1, y1], [x2, y2]], 'c4', true) + curve([[x2, y2], [x1, y1]], 'c4', true); }\n\n  G.econ380Budget = function (a) {\n    var mode = a.mode || 'opt';\n    var o, cap, sx, sy;\n    var ic = function (u) { return function (x) { return u * u / x; }; };\n    if (mode === 'income') {\n      sx = scale(0, 112, X0, X1); sy = scale(0, 44, Y0, Y1);\n      o = axes('x', 'y');\n      o += ln(sx(0), sy(20), sx(50), sy(0), 'c4') + ln(sx(0), sy(40), sx(100), sy(0), 'c4');\n      o += t(X0 - 5, sy(20) + 4, '20', 'lb', 'end') + t(X0 - 5, sy(40) + 4, '40', 'lb', 'end');\n      o += t(sx(50), Y0 + 14, '50', 'lb', 'middle') + t(sx(100), Y0 + 14, '100', 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += curve(plot(ic(Math.sqrt(250)), 250 / 42, 92, sx, sy, 90), 'c1 faint');\n      o += curve(plot(ic(Math.sqrt(1000)), 1000 / 43, 92, sx, sy, 90), 'c1');\n      o += endLabel(sx(92), sy(250 / 92), 'U = 15.8', 'lb') + endLabel(sx(92), sy(1000 / 92) - 3, 'U = 31.6', 'k1');\n      o += ln(sx(0), sy(0), sx(105), sy(42), 'c2 dash');\n      o += t(sx(103), sy(42) - 6, 'expansion path y = 0.4x', 'k2', 'end');\n      o += dot(sx(25), sy(10)) + dot(sx(50), sy(20));\n      o += t(sx(25) + 6, sy(10) + 13, 'A', 'b') + t(sx(50) + 6, sy(20) + 13, 'B', 'b');\n      cap = 'Cobb–Douglas: income doubles from 100 to 200 (budget lines with intercepts 20, 50 and 40, 100) and both goods double, from A (25, 10) to B (50, 20). The optima lie on a ray through the origin: the budget shares never change.';\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: cap };\n    }\n    var top = mode === 'mrs' ? 32 : mode === 'ic' ? 30 : 26;\n    sx = scale(0, 62, X0, X1); sy = scale(0, top, Y0, Y1);\n    o = axes('x', 'y');\n    if (mode === 'mrs') {\n      o += curve(plot(ic(Math.sqrt(250)), 250 / top, 48, sx, sy, 90), 'c1');\n      o += endLabel(sx(48), sy(250 / 48), 'U = 15.8', 'k1');\n      // tangents: slope −y/x\n      o += ln(sx(10 - 3.2), sy(25 + 8), sx(10 + 4), sy(25 - 10), 'c2');\n      o += ln(sx(25 - 12), sy(10 + 4.8), sx(25 + 14), sy(10 - 5.6), 'c2');\n      o += dot(sx(10), sy(25)) + dot(sx(25), sy(10));\n      o += t(sx(10) + 9, sy(25) - 2, 'MRS = 25/10 = 2.5', 'k2');\n      o += t(sx(25) + 8, sy(10) - 12, 'MRS = 10/25 = 0.4', 'k2');\n      cap = 'The MRS is the slope of the indifference curve (ignoring the sign). For U = x<sup>0.5</sup>y<sup>0.5</sup> it is y/x: steep where x is scarce, flat where x is plentiful. That is diminishing MRS.';\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: cap };\n    }\n    var curves = [[10, 'U = 10'], [Math.sqrt(250), mode === 'ic' ? 'U = 15.8' : 'U* = 15.8'], [20, 'U = 20']];\n    curves.forEach(function (u, k) {\n      o += curve(plot(ic(u[0]), u[0] * u[0] / top, 50, sx, sy, 90), k === 1 ? 'c1' : 'c1 faint');\n      o += endLabel(sx(50), sy(u[0] * u[0] / 50), u[1], k === 1 ? 'k1' : 'lb');\n    });\n    if (mode === 'ic') {\n      o += ln(sx(10), sy(25), sx(40), sy(6.25), 'c4 dash');\n      o += dot(sx(10), sy(25)) + dot(sx(40), sy(6.25)) + dot(sx(25), sy(15.625), 'solid2');\n      o += t(sx(10) - 6, sy(25) + 4, 'A', 'b', 'end') + t(sx(40) - 2, sy(6.25) - 8, 'B', 'b', 'end');\n      o += t(sx(25) + 7, sy(15.625) - 5, 'average of A and B', 'k2');\n      o += curve([[sx(3), sy(2)], [sx(9), sy(8)]], 'c4', true);\n      cap = 'Higher curves are better (monotone: the arrow). A (10, 25) and B (40, 6.25) are equally good, but their average (25, 15.6) lies on a higher curve: convex preferences. Curves never cross (transitivity).';\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: cap };\n    }\n    o += ln(sx(0), sy(20), sx(50), sy(0), 'c2');\n    o += t(sx(1.5), sy(20) - 6, 'budget', 'k2');\n    o += vguide(sx(25), sy(10), 'x* = 25', 'k1') + hguide(sx(25), sy(10), '10', 'k1');\n    o += t(X0 - 5, sy(20) + 4, '20', 'lb', 'end') + t(sx(50), Y0 + 14, '50', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += dot(sx(25), sy(10));\n    cap = 'Budget 2x + 5y = 100 (slope −0.4). The best affordable bundle is (25, 10): the indifference curve touches the budget line, so MRS = y/x = 0.4 = p<sub>x</sub>/p<sub>y</sub>. U = 20 is out of reach; U = 10 wastes money.';\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: cap };\n  };\n\n  G.econ380PriceEffects = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 62, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 42, Y0, Y1);\n    var ic = function (k) { return function (x) { return k / x; }; };\n    var o = axes('x', 'y');\n    o += ln(sx(0), sy(20), sx(50), sy(0), 'c4 faint');\n    o += ln(sx(0), sy(20), sx(12.5), sy(0), 'c4');\n    o += ln(sx(0), sy(40), sx(25), sy(0), 'c4 dash');\n    o += t(X0 - 5, sy(20) + 4, '20', 'lb', 'end') + t(X0 - 5, sy(40) + 4, '40', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += curve(plot(ic(250), 250 / 41, 50, sx, sy, 90), 'c1');\n    o += curve(plot(ic(62.5), 62.5 / 41, 50, sx, sy, 90), 'c1 faint');\n    o += endLabel(sx(50), sy(5), 'U₀ = 15.8', 'k1') + endLabel(sx(50), sy(1.25) - 2, 'U₁ = 7.9', 'lb');\n    o += dot(sx(25), sy(10)) + dot(sx(12.5), sy(20)) + dot(sx(6.25), sy(10));\n    o += t(sx(25) + 6, sy(10) - 5, 'A', 'b') + t(sx(12.5) + 6, sy(20) - 4, 'B', 'b') + t(sx(6.25) - 5, sy(10) - 6, 'C', 'b', 'end');\n    o += ln(sx(25), sy(10), sx(25), Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + ln(sx(12.5), sy(20), sx(12.5), Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + ln(sx(6.25), sy(10), sx(6.25), Y0, 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += t(sx(6.25), Y0 + 14, '6.25', 'lb', 'middle') + t(sx(12.5), Y0 + 14, '12.5', 'lb', 'middle') + t(sx(25), Y0 + 14, '25', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[sx(25), Y0 + 26], [sx(12.5) + 3, Y0 + 26]], 'c4', true) + t(sx(25) + 5, Y0 + 30, 'substitution −12.5', 'k2');\n    o += curve([[sx(12.5), Y0 + 42], [sx(6.25) + 3, Y0 + 42]], 'c4', true) + t(sx(12.5) + 5, Y0 + 46, 'income −6.25', 'k3');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 236), cap: 'p<sub>x</sub> rises 2 → 8: the budget line pivots in (solid grey). A → B, along the old indifference curve at the new price ratio (dashed compensated line), is the substitution effect; B → C is the income effect. Total: 25 → 6.25.' };\n  };\n\n  G.econ380Demand = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 55, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('x', 'pₓ');\n    var marsh = [], hick = [];\n    for (var k = 0; k <= 80; k++) {\n      var p = 1.2 + (9.6 - 1.2) * k / 80;\n      marsh.push([sx(50 / p), sy(p)]);\n      hick.push([sx(Math.sqrt(1250 / p)), sy(p)]);\n    }\n    o += curve(marsh, 'c1') + curve(hick, 'c2');\n    o += endLabel(sx(50 / 1.2), sy(1.2), 'x(p, I)', 'k1');\n    o += t(sx(Math.sqrt(1250 / 9.6)) + 5, sy(9.6) + 4, 'h(p, U₀)', 'k2');\n    o += dot(sx(25), sy(2)) + dot(sx(12.5), sy(8)) + dot(sx(6.25), sy(8));\n    o += hguide(sx(12.5), sy(8), '8') + hguide(sx(25), sy(2), '2');\n    o += vguide(sx(6.25), sy(8), '6.25', 'k1') + vguide(sx(12.5), sy(8), '12.5', 'k2') + vguide(sx(25), sy(2), '25');\n    o += t(sx(25) + 6, sy(2) - 7, 'A', 'b');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Ordinary (Marshallian, blue) and compensated (Hicksian, orange) demand both pass through A. At p<sub>x</sub> = 8 compensated demand falls only to 12.5 (substitution); ordinary demand falls to 6.25 (plus the income effect). x is normal, so the Hicksian curve is steeper.' };\n  };\n\n  G.econ380Engel = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('income I', 'quantity');\n    var lux = function (i) { return 0.115 * i * i; }, unit = function (i) { return 0.8 * i; },\n      nec = function (i) { return 3.2 * Math.sqrt(i); }, inf = function (i) { return 1.6 * i - 0.2 * i * i; };\n    o += curve(plot(lux, 0, 8, sx, sy), 'c2') + curve(plot(unit, 0, 8, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += curve(plot(nec, 0, 8, sx, sy), 'c3') + curve(plot(inf, 0, 7.5, sx, sy), 'c4');\n    o += endLabel(sx(8), sy(nec(8)), 'necessity', 'k3') + endLabel(sx(8), sy(lux(8)), 'luxury', 'k2');\n    o += endLabel(sx(8), sy(unit(8)), 'unit', 'k1') + endLabel(sx(7.5), sy(inf(7.5)), 'inferior', 'b');\n    o += ln(sx(4), sy(3.2), sx(4), Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + t(sx(4), Y0 + 14, 'turns inferior', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += dot(sx(4), sy(3.2), 'solid4');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Engel curves: quantity against income. Necessity: 0 &lt; η &lt; 1; unit: η = 1 (Cobb–Douglas, x = I/4 in the running example); luxury: η &gt; 1. The grey good is normal at low incomes and inferior (η &lt; 0) past the peak. A Giffen good is an inferior good with a large budget share.' };\n  };\n\n  G.econ380Elasticity = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 52, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 105, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('Q', 'P');\n    o += ln(sx(0), sy(100), sx(25), sy(50), 'c2') + ln(sx(25), sy(50), sx(50), sy(0), 'c1');\n    o += t(sx(15), sy(86), 'elastic: |ε| > 1', 'k2');\n    o += t(sx(36), sy(44), 'inelastic: |ε| < 1', 'k1');\n    o += dot(sx(20), sy(60)) + t(sx(19), sy(52), '|ε| = 1.5', 'b', 'end');\n    o += dot(sx(25), sy(50)) + t(sx(26.5), sy(52), '|ε| = 1', 'b');\n    o += dot(sx(30), sy(40)) + t(sx(28.5), sy(33), '|ε| = 0.67', 'b', 'end');\n    o += t(X0 - 5, sy(100) + 4, '100', 'lb', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Demand P = 100 − 2Q: one straight line, many elasticities, |ε| = (1/2)(P/Q). Above the midpoint (25, 50) a price cut raises revenue; below it, revenue falls. Revenue peaks at 1,250 where |ε| = 1.' };\n  };\n\n  // --------------------------------------------------------- production\n\n  G.econ380Isoquant = function (a) {\n    var mode = a.mode || 'opt';\n    var o, sx, sy;\n    if (mode === 'scale') {\n      sx = scale(0, 46, X0, X1); sy = scale(0, 100, Y0, Y1);\n      o = axes('L', 'K');\n      o += curve(plot(function (l) { return 400 / l; }, 4, 38, sx, sy, 90), 'c1 faint');\n      o += curve(plot(function (l) { return 1600 / l; }, 16, 38, sx, sy, 90), 'c1');\n      o += endLabel(sx(38), sy(400 / 38), 'Q = 20', 'k1') + endLabel(sx(38), sy(1600 / 38), 'Q = 40', 'k1');\n      o += ln(sx(0), sy(0), sx(25), sy(100), 'c4 dash');\n      o += t(sx(16.5), sy(70), 'ray K = 4L', 'lb', 'end');\n      o += dot(sx(10), sy(40)) + dot(sx(20), sy(80));\n      o += t(sx(10) + 6, sy(40) + 2, '(10, 40)', 'b') + t(sx(20) + 6, sy(80) + 2, '(20, 80)', 'b');\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Q = K<sup>0.5</sup>L<sup>0.5</sup>: doubling both inputs, (10, 40) → (20, 80), moves from the Q = 20 isoquant to Q = 40. Output doubles too: constant returns to scale.' };\n    }\n    sx = scale(0, 34, X0, X1); sy = scale(0, 85, Y0, Y1);\n    o = axes('L', 'K');\n    o += curve(plot(function (l) { return 400 / l; }, 400 / 84, 28, sx, sy, 90), 'c1');\n    o += endLabel(sx(28), sy(400 / 28), 'Q = 20', 'k1');\n    if (mode === 'mrts') {\n      o += ln(sx(10 - 4), sy(40 + 16), sx(10 + 5), sy(40 - 20), 'c2');\n      o += ln(sx(20 - 8), sy(20 + 8), sx(20 + 7), sy(20 - 7), 'c2');\n      o += dot(sx(10), sy(40)) + dot(sx(20), sy(20));\n      o += t(sx(11.5), sy(46), 'MRTS = 40/10 = 4', 'k2');\n      o += t(sx(19), sy(9), 'MRTS = 20/20 = 1', 'k2', 'end');\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'The slope of the isoquant is the MRTS = MP<sub>L</sub>/MP<sub>K</sub> = K/L. With lots of capital per worker, one more worker replaces 4 units of K; with less capital, only 1.' };\n    }\n    o += ln(sx(0), sy(80), sx(20), sy(0), 'c2');\n    o += t(sx(20) + 4, Y0 - 6, 'isocost 4L + K = 80', 'k2');\n    o += vguide(sx(10), sy(40), 'L* = 10', 'k1') + hguide(sx(10), sy(40), '40', 'k1');\n    o += t(X0 - 5, sy(80) + 4, '80', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += dot(sx(10), sy(40));\n    o += dot(sx(20), sy(20), 'solid4') + t(sx(21), sy(28), '(20, 20) costs $100', 'lb');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Cheapest way to make 20 units: the lowest isocost line (slope −w/r = −4) that touches the isoquant, at L = 10, K = 40, where MRTS = K/L = 4 = w/r. Cost $80.' };\n  };\n\n  // the running firm: TC = 32 + q³ − 6q² + 20q\n  var MC = function (q) { return 3 * q * q - 12 * q + 20; };\n  var AVC = function (q) { return q * q - 6 * q + 20; };\n  var ATC = function (q) { return AVC(q) + 32 / q; };\n\n  G.econ380Costs = function (a) {\n    var shut = a.mode === 'shutdown';\n    var sx = scale(0, 7, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 60, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('q', '$');\n    o += curve(plot(ATC, 0.75, 6.3, sx, sy), 'c1') + curve(plot(AVC, 0, 6.3, sx, sy), 'c1 faint');\n    o += endLabel(sx(6.3), sy(ATC(6.3)), 'ATC', 'k1') + endLabel(sx(6.3), sy(AVC(6.3)) + 2, 'AVC', 'k1');\n    if (shut) {\n      o += curve(plot(MC, 0.5, 3, sx, sy), 'c2 faint') + curve(plot(MC, 3, 6, sx, sy), 'c3');\n      o += endLabel(sx(6), sy(MC(6)), 'supply', 'k3');\n      o += ln(X0, sy(20), X1, sy(20), 'c4 dash faint') + ln(X0, sy(11), X1, sy(11), 'c4 dash faint');\n      o += t(X0 - 5, sy(20) + 4, '20', 'lb', 'end') + t(X0 - 5, sy(11) + 4, '11', 'lb', 'end');\n      o += t(X0 + 4, sy(20) - 5, 'break-even', 'lb') + t(X0 + 4, sy(11) + 12, 'shutdown', 'lb');\n      o += hguide(sx(5), sy(35), '35') + vguide(sx(5), sy(35), '5');\n      o += dot(sx(3), sy(11)) + dot(sx(4), sy(20)) + dot(sx(5), sy(35));\n      o += t(sx(3), Y0 + 14, '3', 'lb', 'middle') + t(sx(4), Y0 + 14, '4', 'lb', 'middle');\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'The firm supplies along MC above min AVC = 11 (green). At P = 35 it makes q = 5 and a profit of 68. Between 11 and 20 it loses money, but less than its fixed cost of 32; below 11 it shuts down.' };\n    }\n    o += curve(plot(MC, 0.5, 6, sx, sy), 'c2') + endLabel(sx(6), sy(MC(6)), 'MC', 'k2');\n    o += dot(sx(3), sy(11)) + dot(sx(4), sy(20));\n    o += vguide(sx(3), sy(11), '3') + vguide(sx(4), sy(20), '4') + hguide(sx(3), sy(11), '11') + hguide(sx(4), sy(20), '20');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'TC = 32 + q³ − 6q² + 20q. MC (orange) cuts AVC at its minimum (3, 11) and ATC at its minimum (4, 20). The gap between ATC and AVC is average fixed cost, 32/q, which shrinks as q grows.' };\n  };\n\n  G.econ380LongRun = function () {\n    // left: the firm; right: the market\n    var fx = scale(0, 7, 42, 148), fy = scale(0, 50, Y0, Y1);\n    var mx = scale(0, 1000, 194, 304), my = fy;\n    var o = panelAxes(42, Y0, 148, Y1, 'q', '$') + panelAxes(194, Y0, 304, Y1, 'Q', 'P');\n    o += curve(plot(MC, 0.6, 5, fx, fy), 'c2') + curve(plot(ATC, 0.9, 6.4, fx, fy), 'c1');\n    o += t(fx(5) - 5, fy(MC(5)) + 4, 'MC', 'k2', 'end') + t(fx(6.4) + 3, fy(ATC(6.4)) + 4, 'ATC', 'k1');\n    o += ln(42, fy(20), 148, fy(20), 'c4 dash') + dot(fx(4), fy(20));\n    o += ln(fx(4), fy(20), fx(4), Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + t(fx(4), Y0 + 14, 'q = 4', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += t(37, fy(20) + 4, '20', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(95, Y0 + 28, 'one firm', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += ln(mx(0), my(50), mx(1000), my(0), 'c1') + t(mx(1000), my(0) - 6, 'D', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += ln(194, my(20), 304, my(20), 'c3') + t(198, my(20) + 14, 'LR supply', 'k3');\n    o += dot(mx(600), my(20)) + ln(mx(600), my(20), mx(600), Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + t(mx(600), Y0 + 14, '600', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += t(249, Y0 + 28, 'market: 150 firms', 'lb', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 214), cap: 'Long run: entry and exit set P = min ATC = 20. Each firm makes q = 4 at zero profit; demand Q = 1,000 − 20P decides how many firms there are (600 / 4 = 150).' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ markets\n\n  var Dm = function (q) { return 100 - 2 * q; }, Sm = function (q) { return 10 + q; };\n\n  G.econ380Surplus = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 52, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 105, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('Q', 'P');\n    o += area([[sx(0), sy(100)], [sx(30), sy(40)], [sx(0), sy(40)]], 'fill1');\n    o += area([[sx(0), sy(40)], [sx(30), sy(40)], [sx(0), sy(10)]], 'fill3');\n    o += curve(plot(Dm, 0, 50, sx, sy), 'c1') + curve(plot(Sm, 0, 52, sx, sy), 'c3');\n    o += t(sx(50), Y0 - 6, 'D', 'k1', 'end') + t(sx(52), sy(62) - 8, 'S', 'k3', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(9), sy(52), 'CS = 900', 'k1', 'middle') + t(sx(9), sy(33), 'PS = 450', 'k3', 'middle');\n    o += vguide(sx(30), sy(40), 'Q* = 30') + hguide(sx(30), sy(40), '40');\n    o += t(X0 - 5, sy(100) + 4, '100', 'lb', 'end') + t(X0 - 5, sy(10) + 4, '10', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += dot(sx(30), sy(40));\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Demand P = 100 − 2Q, supply P = 10 + Q. Consumer surplus (blue) lies under demand and above the price; producer surplus (green) above supply and below it. Total surplus at the equilibrium, 1,350, is the most possible.' };\n  };\n\n  G.econ380Tax = function (a) {\n    var inc = a.mode === 'incidence';\n    var sx = scale(0, 40, X0, X1), sy = scale(25, 55, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('Q', 'P') + brk(X0, Y0 - 14);\n    o += rect(sx(0), sy(44), sx(28) - sx(0), sy(38) - sy(44), 'fill2', 0);\n    o += area([[sx(28), sy(44)], [sx(28), sy(38)], [sx(30), sy(40)]], 'fill1');\n    o += curve(plot(Dm, 22.5, 37.5, sx, sy), 'c1') + curve(plot(Sm, 15, 40, sx, sy), 'c3');\n    o += curve(plot(function (q) { return Sm(q) + 6; }, 9, 39, sx, sy), 'c3 dash');\n    o += t(sx(22.5) + 6, sy(55) + 10, 'D', 'k1');\n    o += t(sx(40), sy(50) + 14, 'S', 'k3', 'end') + t(sx(37.5), sy(55) + 10, 'S + $6', 'k3', 'end');\n    o += hguide(sx(28), sy(44), '44', 'k1') + hguide(sx(28), sy(38), '38', 'k3');\n    o += ln(sx(28), sy(38), sx(28), Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + ln(sx(30), sy(40), sx(30), Y0, 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += t(sx(28) - 2, Y0 + 14, '28', 'lb', 'end') + t(sx(30) + 2, Y0 + 14, '30', 'lb', 'start');\n    o += dot(sx(28), sy(44)) + dot(sx(28), sy(38)) + dot(sx(30), sy(40), 'solid4');\n    o += t(sx(13), sy(41) + 4, 'revenue 6 × 28 = 168', 'k2', 'middle');\n    if (inc) {\n      o += curve([[sx(33), sy(40)], [sx(33), sy(43.8)]], 'c4', true) + t(sx(33) + 5, sy(42) + 4, 'buyers +4', 'k1');\n      o += curve([[sx(33), sy(40)], [sx(33), sy(38.2)]], 'c4', true) + t(sx(33) + 5, sy(37.5) + 6, 'sellers −2', 'k3');\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'The $6 wedge: buyers pay 44 (up 4 from 40), sellers keep 38 (down 2). Demand is the less elastic side here, so buyers bear two-thirds, whoever writes the check.' };\n    }\n    o += t(sx(23.5), sy(27.5), 'DWL = 6', 'k1') + ln(sx(27), sy(30), sx(28.6), sy(39.3), 'c4');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'A $6 tax (the market was at Q = 30, P = 40): quantity falls to 28. The orange rectangle is revenue, a transfer to government; the blue triangle is the deadweight loss, ½ × 6 × 2 = 6, from the 2 units no longer traded.' };\n  };\n\n  G.econ380Ceiling = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 52, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 105, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('Q', 'P');\n    o += area([[sx(24), sy(52)], [sx(24), sy(34)], [sx(30), sy(40)]], 'fill2');\n    o += curve(plot(Dm, 0, 50, sx, sy), 'c1') + curve(plot(Sm, 0, 52, sx, sy), 'c3');\n    o += t(sx(50), Y0 - 6, 'D', 'k1', 'end') + t(sx(52), sy(62) - 8, 'S', 'k3', 'end');\n    o += ln(X0, sy(34), X1, sy(34), 'c2') + t(X1, sy(34) + 13, 'ceiling $34', 'k2', 'end');\n    o += dot(sx(24), sy(34)) + dot(sx(33), sy(34)) + dot(sx(30), sy(40), 'solid4');\n    o += ln(sx(24), sy(52), sx(24), Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + ln(sx(33), sy(34), sx(33), Y0, 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += t(sx(24) - 2, Y0 + 14, sb('Q', 'S', ' = 24'), 'lb', 'end') + t(sx(33) + 2, Y0 + 14, sb('Q', 'D', ' = 33'), 'lb', 'start');\n    o += both(sx(24) + 3, sy(22), sx(33) - 3, sy(22));\n    o += t(sx(28.5), sy(22) + 13, 'shortage 9', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += hguide(sx(24), sy(52), '52') + dot(sx(24), sy(52), 'solid4') + t(X0 - 5, sy(34) + 4, '34', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(22.5), sy(43), 'DWL = 54', 'k2', 'end') + ln(sx(22.6), sy(42), sx(25.5), sy(42), 'c4');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'A $34 ceiling (equilibrium $40): sellers offer 24, buyers want 33. The 6 lost units were worth up to $52 to buyers but cost only $34 or more to make: DWL = ½ × 6 × 18 = 54.' };\n  };\n\n  G.econ380Edgeworth = function (a) {\n    var L = 78, R = 248, T = 18, B = 188;      // the box, 10 × 10\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, L, R), sy = scale(0, 10, B, T);\n    var o = rect(L, T, R - L, B - T, 'box', 0);\n    o += ln(sx(0), sy(0), sx(10), sy(10), 'c3 dash');\n    var cx = sx(2.4) - 4, cy = sy(2.4) - 4;\n    o += '<text x=\"' + K.r(cx) + '\" y=\"' + K.r(cy) + '\" class=\"k3\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-45 ' + K.r(cx) + ' ' + K.r(cy) + ')\">contract curve</text>';\n    var iA = function (k) { return plot(function (x) { return k / x; }, k / 10, 10, sx, sy, 70); };\n    var iB = function (k) { return plot(function (x) { return 10 - k / (10 - x); }, 0, 10 - k / 10, sx, sy, 70); };\n    o += curve(iA(16), 'c1') + curve(iB(16), 'c2');\n    o += curve(iA(25), 'c1 faint') + curve(iB(25), 'c2 faint');\n    if (a.mode === 'ce') o += ln(sx(10), sy(0), sx(0), sy(10), 'c4');\n    o += dot(sx(8), sy(2)) + t(sx(8) - 6, sy(2) + 14, 'E', 'b', 'end');\n    o += dot(sx(5), sy(5)) + t(sx(6), sy(9.2), a.mode === 'ce' ? 'CE (5, 5)' : '(5, 5)', 'b') + ln(sx(6.3), sy(8.9), sx(5.15), sy(5.35), 'c4 faint');\n    o += t(L - 4, B + 12, sb('O', 'A'), 'b', 'end') + t(R + 4, T - 4, sb('O', 'B'), 'b', 'start');\n    o += t(R, B + 14, sb('x', 'A', ' →'), 'it', 'end') + t(L - 6, T + 10, sb('y', 'A', ' ↑'), 'it', 'end');\n    o += t(L, T - 5, sb('← x', 'B'), 'it', 'start') + t(R + 6, T + 22, sb('y', 'B', ' ↓'), 'it', 'start');\n    o += t(R + 6, sy(1.6) + 4, sb('U', 'A'), 'k1') + t(L - 6, sy(8.4) + 4, sb('U', 'B'), 'k2', 'end');\n    var cap = a.mode === 'ce'\n      ? 'Two people, 10 of each good. At prices 1 : 1 the budget line (black) runs through the endowment E (8, 2); both choose (5, 5), where it is tangent to both indifference curves. The competitive equilibrium is on the contract curve.'\n      : 'Two people, 10 of each good; A starts at E (8, 2). Through E, A’s (blue) and B’s (orange) indifference curves cross: the lens between them holds trades both prefer. Efficient points, where the curves are tangent, form the contract curve.';\n    return { svg: svg(o, 212), cap: cap };\n  };\n\n  // ---------------------------------------------------------- monopoly\n\n  G.econ380Monopoly = function (a) {\n    var lerner = a.mode === 'lerner';\n    var sx = scale(0, 52, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 105, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('Q', '$');\n    if (!lerner) {\n      o += rect(sx(0), sy(60), sx(20) - sx(0), sy(20) - sy(60), 'fill1', 0);\n      o += area([[sx(20), sy(60)], [sx(20), sy(20)], [sx(40), sy(20)]], 'fill2');\n    }\n    o += curve(plot(Dm, 0, 50, sx, sy), 'c1') + curve(plot(function (q) { return 100 - 4 * q; }, 0, 25, sx, sy), 'c1 dash');\n    o += ln(X0, sy(20), X1, sy(20), 'c2');\n    o += t(sx(50), Y0 - 6, 'D', 'k1', 'end') + t(sx(25) + 4, Y0 - 6, 'MR', 'k1');\n    o += t(X1, sy(20) - 6, 'MC = 20', 'k2', 'end');\n    o += dot(sx(20), sy(20)) + dot(sx(20), sy(60));\n    o += vguide(sx(20), sy(60), '20') + hguide(sx(20), sy(60), '60', 'k1') + t(X0 - 5, sy(20) + 4, '20', 'lb', 'end');\n    if (lerner) {\n      o += both(sx(22), sy(21.5), sx(22), sy(58.5));\n      o += t(sx(30), sy(88), 'P − MC = 40', 'k2');\n      o += t(sx(30), sy(88) + 14, 'L = 40/60 = 2/3', 'b');\n      o += t(sx(30), sy(88) + 28, '|ε| = 1.5 here', 'k1');\n      o += ln(sx(29.5), sy(68), sx(22.8), sy(45), 'c4 faint');\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'At the monopoly point (20, 60), |ε| = (1/2)(60/20) = 1.5 and the markup is (P − MC)/P = 2/3 = 1/|ε|. The monopolist is on the elastic part of demand.' };\n    }\n    o += t(sx(6), sy(40), 'profit 800', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(26), sy(31), 'DWL 400', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += vguide(sx(40), sy(20), '40') + dot(sx(40), sy(20), 'solid4');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Demand P = 100 − 2Q, MR = 100 − 4Q (dashed). MR = MC at Q = 20; the price comes from the demand curve, 60. Blue: profit (60 − 20) × 20 = 800. Orange: the deadweight loss of the units competition (Q = 40) would have sold.' };\n  };\n\n  G.econ380PriceDisc = function () {\n    var ax = scale(0, 50, 42, 160), bx = scale(0, 50, 190, 308), sy = scale(0, 105, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = panelAxes(42, Y0, 160, Y1, 'Q', '$') + panelAxes(190, Y0, 308, Y1, 'Q', '$');\n    // adults\n    o += ln(ax(0), sy(100), ax(50), sy(0), 'c1') + ln(ax(0), sy(100), ax(25), sy(0), 'c1 dash');\n    o += ln(42, sy(20), 160, sy(20), 'c2');\n    o += dot(ax(20), sy(60)) + ln(ax(20), sy(60), ax(20), Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + ln(42, sy(60), ax(20), sy(60), 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += t(ax(20) + 5, sy(60) - 5, '(20, 60)', 'b');\n    o += t(101, Y1 + 4, 'Adults', 'b', 'middle') + t(ax(50), Y0 + 14, 'D₁', 'k1', 'middle') + t(ax(25), Y0 + 14, 'MR₁', 'k1', 'middle');\n    // students\n    o += ln(bx(0), sy(60), bx(30), sy(0), 'c1') + ln(bx(0), sy(60), bx(15), sy(0), 'c1 dash');\n    o += ln(190, sy(20), 308, sy(20), 'c2') + t(308, sy(20) - 5, 'MC = 20', 'k2', 'end');\n    o += dot(bx(10), sy(40)) + ln(bx(10), sy(40), bx(10), Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + ln(190, sy(40), bx(10), sy(40), 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += t(bx(10) + 5, sy(40) - 5, '(10, 40)', 'b');\n    o += t(249, Y1 + 4, 'Students', 'b', 'middle') + t(bx(30), Y0 + 14, 'D₂', 'k1', 'middle') + t(bx(15), Y0 + 14, 'MR₂', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += t(37, sy(20) + 4, '20', 'lb', 'end') + t(37, sy(60) + 4, '60', 'lb', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 200), cap: 'Third-degree discrimination: MR = MC = 20 in each market. Adults (P = 100 − 2Q; |ε| = 1.5 at the optimum) pay 60; students (P = 60 − 2Q; |ε| = 2, more elastic) pay 40.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------- games\n\n  G.econ380Matrix = function (a) {\n    var game = a.game || 'pd';\n    var g = game === 'coord'\n      ? { rows: ['X', 'Y'], cols: ['X', 'Y'], A: [[2, 0], [0, 1]], B: [[2, 0], [0, 1]], p1: 'Player 1', p2: 'Player 2' }\n      : { rows: ['High', 'Low'], cols: ['High', 'Low'], A: [[10, 2], [12, 5]], B: [[10, 12], [2, 5]], p1: 'Firm A', p2: 'Firm B' };\n    var x0 = 112, y0 = 50, cw = 92, ch = 52;\n    var o = t(x0 + cw, 16, g.p2, 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(18, y0 + ch + 4, g.p1, 'k1', 'start');\n    for (var j = 0; j < 2; j++) o += t(x0 + cw * j + cw / 2, y0 - 10, g.cols[j], 'b', 'middle');\n    for (var i = 0; i < 2; i++) {\n      o += t(x0 - 8, y0 + ch * i + ch / 2 + 4, g.rows[i], 'b', 'end');\n      for (j = 0; j < 2; j++) {\n        var rBR = g.A[i][j] === Math.max(g.A[0][j], g.A[1][j]);\n        var cBR = g.B[i][j] === Math.max(g.B[i][0], g.B[i][1]);\n        var x = x0 + cw * j, y = y0 + ch * i;\n        o += rect(x, y, cw, ch, rBR && cBR ? 'box3' : 'box', 0);\n        if (a.show === 'coop' && i === 0 && j === 0) o += rect(x + 4, y + 4, cw - 8, ch - 8, 'c4 dash', 4);\n        var cx = x + cw / 2, cy = y + ch / 2 + 5;\n        o += t(cx - 6, cy, String(g.A[i][j]), rBR ? 'k1' : 'lb', 'end') + t(cx, cy, ',', 'lb', 'middle') + t(cx + 6, cy, String(g.B[i][j]), cBR ? 'k2' : 'lb', 'start');\n        if (rBR) o += ln(cx - 22, cy + 4, cx - 6, cy + 4, 'c1');\n        if (cBR) o += ln(cx + 6, cy + 4, cx + 22, cy + 4, 'c2');\n      }\n    }\n    var note = game === 'coord' ? 'two equilibria: (X, X) and (Y, Y)' : 'Nash equilibrium: (Low, Low)';\n    o += t(x0 + cw, y0 + 2 * ch + 22, note, 'k3', 'middle');\n    if (a.show === 'coop') o += t(x0 + cw, y0 + 2 * ch + 38, 'dashed: (High, High) is better for both', 'lb', 'middle');\n    var cap = game === 'coord'\n      ? 'Underlined payoffs are best responses (blue for player 1, orange for player 2). Green cells, where both are best responses, are Nash equilibria. No player has a dominant strategy here.'\n      : 'Payoffs (A, B) in $m. Underlined = best response. Low is best for A in both columns and for B in both rows: a dominant strategy. The green cell is the equilibrium.';\n    return { svg: svg(o, a.show === 'coop' ? 206 : 190), cap: cap };\n  };\n\n\n  G.econ380Cournot = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 132, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 128, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('q₁', 'q₂');\n    o += ln(sx(60), sy(0), sx(0), sy(120), 'c1') + ln(sx(0), sy(60), sx(120), sy(0), 'c2');\n    o += t(sx(9), sy(112), 'firm 1: q₁ = (120 − q₂)/2', 'k1');\n    o += t(sx(122), sy(34), 'firm 2: q₂ = (120 − q₁)/2', 'k2', 'end');\n    o += dot(sx(40), sy(40)) + t(sx(46), sy(45), 'Cournot (40, 40)', 'b');\n    o += dot(sx(30), sy(30), 'solid4') + t(sx(27), sy(22), 'collusion', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(X0 - 5, sy(60) + 4, '60', 'lb', 'end') + t(X0 - 5, sy(120) + 4, '120', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(60), Y0 + 14, '60', 'lb', 'middle') + t(sx(120), Y0 + 14, '120', 'lb', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'P = 120 − Q, MC = 0. The best-response lines cross at (40, 40): Q = 80, P = 40. Splitting monopoly output, (30, 30), would pay each more, but each firm’s best response to 30 is 45.' };\n  };\n\n  G.econ380Outcomes = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 128, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 128, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('Q', 'P');\n    o += ln(sx(0), sy(120), sx(120), sy(0), 'c1') + t(sx(4), sy(100), 'D', 'k1');\n    o += t(X0 + 4, Y0 - 5, 'MC = 0', 'k2');\n    o += dot(sx(60), sy(60)) + t(sx(62), sy(60) - 7, 'monopoly', 'b');\n    o += dot(sx(80), sy(40)) + t(sx(82), sy(40) - 7, 'Cournot', 'b');\n    o += dot(sx(120), sy(0)) + t(sx(120) + 2, Y0 + 14, 'Bertrand', 'b', 'end');\n    o += hguide(sx(60), sy(60), '60') + hguide(sx(80), sy(40), '40') + t(X0 - 5, sy(120) + 4, '120', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(60), Y0 + 14, '60', 'lb', 'middle') + t(sx(80), Y0 + 14, '80', 'lb', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Same demand P = 120 − Q and MC = 0, three market structures. Monopoly (60, 60); Cournot (80, 40) lies between monopoly and competition; Bertrand price-cutting with identical goods drives P to MC (120, 0).' };\n  };\n})();\n";
// The review page itself, shared with the website build (tools/page/).
const PAGE = {"css":"  :root { --ink:#2B2118; --cream:#FDF8F2; --wk:#1F4E8C; --wk-soft:rgba(31,78,140,.10); }\n  * { box-sizing:border-box; -webkit-tap-highlight-color:transparent; }\n  html, body { margin:0; height:100%; overflow:hidden;\n    background:var(--cream); color:var(--ink);\n    font-family:-apple-system,system-ui,sans-serif; user-select:none; -webkit-user-select:none; }\n  #app { height:100%; display:flex; flex-direction:column; }\n  header { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:10px; padding:12px 16px 2px; }\n  #quitline { text-align:center; padding-bottom:2px; }\n  #bar { flex:1; height:5px; border-radius:3px; background:rgba(0,0,0,.09); overflow:hidden; }\n  #fill { height:100%; width:0%; background:var(--wk); transition:width .25s ease, background .25s; }\n  #count { font-size:12px; opacity:.55; font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums; }\n  #quit { font-size:11px; opacity:.45; }\n  #logo { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:6px; padding:4px 6px 4px 2px;\n          font-size:13px; font-weight:800; letter-spacing:.06em; color:var(--wk); transition:color .25s; }\n  #logo svg { width:19px; height:19px; }\n  main { flex:1; position:relative; display:flex; min-height:0; }\n  /* margin:auto centres the card while it is short but still lets it scroll\n     once the answer is taller than the screen. */\n  #scroll { flex:1; display:flex; overflow-y:auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;\n            padding:14px 20px 18px; }\n  #card { margin:auto; width:100%; max-width:560px; text-align:center; }\n\n  .chips { display:flex; justify-content:center; gap:6px; flex-wrap:wrap; }\n  .chip { font-size:10.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.08em; text-transform:uppercase;\n          padding:4px 9px; border-radius:20px; background:var(--wk-soft); color:var(--wk); }\n  .chip.star { background:rgba(194,98,42,.13); color:#C2622A; }\n  .term { font-family:ui-serif,\"New York\",Georgia,serif; font-weight:700;\n          font-size:36px; line-height:1.18; margin-top:18px; letter-spacing:-.01em; }\n  .weekname { font-size:13px; opacity:.5; margin-top:12px; }\n  .term.sm { font-size:21px; margin-top:12px; color:var(--wk); }\n  .simple { font-size:20px; line-height:1.42; margin-top:10px; }\n  .fx { margin:14px auto 0; padding:12px 14px; border-radius:14px; background:var(--wk-soft);\n        font-family:ui-serif,\"New York\",Georgia,serif; font-size:19px; line-height:1.6;\n        display:inline-block; max-width:100%; }\n  .fx i, .simple i, .detail i, .peek i { font-family:ui-serif,\"New York\",Georgia,serif; }\n\n  /* hand-built maths: fractions, sums, hats */\n  .fr { display:inline-flex; flex-direction:column; vertical-align:middle; text-align:center;\n        margin:0 2px; line-height:1.15; }\n  .fr > span:first-child { border-bottom:1.3px solid currentColor; padding:0 3px 1px; }\n  .fr > span:last-child { padding:1px 3px 0; }\n  .fx .fr { font-size:.92em; }\n  .op { margin:0 .22em; }\n  sub, sup { font-size:.68em; line-height:0; }\n\n  .detail { margin-top:18px; text-align:start; }\n  .sect { border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.09); margin-top:13px; padding-top:10px; }\n  .sect h3 { margin:0 0 6px; font-size:10.5px; font-weight:700; opacity:.45;\n             letter-spacing:.09em; text-transform:uppercase; }\n  .sect p { margin:0; font-size:14px; line-height:1.48; opacity:.86; }\n  .sect p + p { margin-top:6px; }\n  .row { display:flex; align-items:baseline; justify-content:space-between;\n         gap:14px; padding:4px 0; }\n  .row .lbl { font-size:13px; opacity:.65; line-height:1.35; }\n  .row .val { font-size:16px; font-weight:700; white-space:nowrap; flex:none;\n              color:var(--wk); font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums; }\n  .src { font-size:11px; opacity:.42; margin-top:4px; line-height:1.35; }\n  ol.steps { margin:0; padding-left:20px; font-size:14px; line-height:1.48; }\n  ol.steps li { padding:2px 0; opacity:.88; }\n  ol.steps li::marker { color:var(--wk); font-weight:700; }\n  .links { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:6px; }\n  .link { font-size:13px; padding:6px 11px; border-radius:20px; border:1px solid var(--wk-soft);\n          background:#fff; color:var(--ink); line-height:1.2; }\n  .link b { color:var(--wk); font-weight:600; }\n  .graph { margin-top:4px; background:#fff; border-radius:14px; padding:8px 6px 4px;\n           border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.06); }\n  .graph svg { width:100%; height:auto; display:block; overflow:visible; }\n  .graph .cap { font-size:11.5px; opacity:.55; text-align:center; padding:2px 8px 6px; line-height:1.35; }\n  .tags { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:5px; justify-content:center; margin-top:18px; }\n  .tag { font-size:10.5px; padding:2px 8px; border-radius:20px; border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.15); opacity:.6; }\n  .tag.pe { border-color:#E8A25E; color:#B4561B; background:#FFF3E6; opacity:1; font-weight:700; }\n\n  /* SVG theme */\n  svg .ax { stroke:var(--ink); stroke-width:1.3; fill:none; opacity:.75; }\n  svg .grid { stroke:var(--ink); stroke-width:.6; opacity:.12; }\n  svg .c1 { stroke:var(--wk); stroke-width:2.6; fill:none; stroke-linecap:round; }\n  svg .c2 { stroke:#C2622A; stroke-width:2.6; fill:none; stroke-linecap:round; }\n  svg .c3 { stroke:#2E7D52; stroke-width:2.4; fill:none; stroke-linecap:round; }\n  svg .c4 { stroke:var(--ink); stroke-width:1.8; fill:none; opacity:.55; stroke-linecap:round; }\n  svg .dash { stroke-dasharray:4 4; }\n  svg .faint { opacity:.4; }\n  svg .dot { fill:var(--ink); }\n  svg .fill1 { fill:var(--wk); opacity:.16; }\n  svg .fill2 { fill:#C2622A; opacity:.16; }\n  svg .fill3 { fill:#2E7D52; opacity:.16; }\n  svg .solid1 { fill:var(--wk); }\n  svg .solid2 { fill:#C2622A; }\n  svg .solid3 { fill:#2E7D52; }\n  svg .solid4 { fill:var(--ink); opacity:.35; }\n  svg .box { fill:#fff; stroke:var(--ink); stroke-width:1.2; }\n  svg .box1 { fill:var(--wk-soft); stroke:var(--wk); stroke-width:1.4; }\n  svg .box2 { fill:rgba(194,98,42,.10); stroke:#C2622A; stroke-width:1.4; }\n  svg .box3 { fill:rgba(46,125,82,.10); stroke:#2E7D52; stroke-width:1.4; }\n  svg text { font-family:-apple-system,system-ui,sans-serif; font-size:11px; fill:var(--ink); }\n  svg text.lb { font-size:10px; opacity:.6; }\n  svg text.k1 { fill:var(--wk); font-weight:700; }\n  svg text.k2 { fill:#C2622A; font-weight:700; }\n  svg text.k3 { fill:#2E7D52; font-weight:700; }\n  svg text.it { font-family:ui-serif,\"New York\",Georgia,serif; font-style:italic; font-size:12.5px; }\n  svg text.b { font-weight:700; }\n  svg text.w { fill:#fff; font-weight:700; }\n\n  #tip { flex:none; text-align:center; font-size:12px; opacity:.45; padding:0 16px 8px; }\n  #flash { position:absolute; inset:0; display:flex; align-items:center;\n           justify-content:center; font-size:34px; font-weight:800;\n           color:#fff; opacity:0; pointer-events:none; transition:opacity .18s; }\n  #flash div { text-align:center; }\n  #flash small { display:block; font-size:15px; font-weight:600; opacity:.9; margin-top:4px; }\n  footer { display:flex; gap:9px; padding:0 14px 26px; }\n  .btn { flex:1; padding:14px 0 12px; border-radius:15px; font-size:15.5px;\n         font-weight:700; color:#fff; text-align:center; line-height:1.15; }\n  .btn small { display:block; font-size:10.5px; font-weight:600; opacity:.8; margin-top:2px; }\n  .missed { background:#C0392B; }\n  .shaky  { background:#D9952B; }\n  .confident { background:#2E7D52; }\n\n  #peek { position:absolute; left:0; right:0; bottom:0; background:#fff; border-radius:20px 20px 0 0;\n          box-shadow:0 -10px 30px rgba(0,0,0,.14); padding:18px 22px 34px; transform:translateY(105%);\n          transition:transform .22s ease, visibility 0s linear .22s; text-align:center; max-height:70%;\n          overflow-y:auto; z-index:5; visibility:hidden; pointer-events:none; }\n  /* Hidden, it sits just below the card, over the grade buttons: it must not\n     catch their taps. */\n  #peek.on { transform:none; visibility:visible; pointer-events:auto; transition:transform .22s ease; }\n  #peek .grab { width:38px; height:5px; border-radius:3px; background:rgba(0,0,0,.15); margin:-6px auto 12px; }\n  #peek .term { font-size:22px; margin-top:8px; }\n  #peek .simple { font-size:16px; }\n  #peek .fx { font-size:16px; }\n  #peek .close { margin-top:16px; font-size:13px; opacity:.5; }\n\n  #done { display:none; flex-direction:column; align-items:center;\n          justify-content:center; height:100%; gap:12px; padding:0 30px; text-align:center; }\n  #done h2 { font-family:ui-serif,\"New York\",Georgia,serif; font-size:30px; margin:0; }\n  #done p { opacity:.6; margin:0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.45; }\n  #done .tally { display:flex; gap:10px; margin:6px 0; }\n  #done .tally div { padding:10px 14px; border-radius:14px; color:#fff; font-weight:700; font-size:20px; min-width:74px; }\n  #done .tally small { display:block; font-size:10.5px; font-weight:600; opacity:.85; }\n","body":"<div id=\"app\">\n  <header>\n    <span id=\"logo\">\n      <svg viewBox=\"0 0 40 40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">\n        <path d=\"M6 5v29h29\" stroke-width=\"2.6\"/>\n        <path d=\"M10 29c6-1 9-5 12-11s6-9 11-10\" stroke-width=\"2.6\"/>\n        <path d=\"M10 12l23 17\" stroke-width=\"2\" opacity=\".55\"/>\n      </svg>ECON 380\n    </span>\n    <div id=\"bar\"><div id=\"fill\"></div></div>\n    <span id=\"count\"></span>\n  </header>\n  <div id=\"quitline\"><span id=\"quit\">__QUIT__</span></div>\n  <main>\n    <div id=\"scroll\"><div id=\"card\"></div></div>\n    <div id=\"flash\"></div>\n    <div id=\"peek\"></div>\n  </main>\n  <div id=\"tip\">Tap to flip</div>\n  <footer>\n    <div class=\"btn missed\" data-act=\"missed\">Missed<small>again now</small></div>\n    <div class=\"btn shaky\" data-act=\"shaky\">Shaky<small>in 1 hour</small></div>\n    <div class=\"btn confident\" data-act=\"confident\">Confident<small id=\"confHint\">later</small></div>\n  </footer>\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"done\">\n  <h2>Done</h2>\n  <p id=\"doneText\"></p>\n  <div class=\"tally\">\n    <div class=\"missed\"><span id=\"tM\">0</span><small>missed</small></div>\n    <div class=\"shaky\"><span id=\"tS\">0</span><small>shaky</small></div>\n    <div class=\"confident\"><span id=\"tC\">0</span><small>confident</small></div>\n  </div>\n  <p id=\"laterText\"></p>\n  __DONE_ACTIONS__\n</div>\n","js":"// The review page shared by the phone (Scriptable) and the website. Each\n// host fills these in: the phone injects one session's cards when it builds\n// the page; the website starts with none and calls startSession() from its\n// menu.\nlet CARDS = /*__CARDS__*/[];\nconst WEEKS = /*__WEEKS__*/[];\nlet PRACTICE = /*__PRACTICE__*/false;\n// Page text and behaviour that differ between a touch screen and a keyboard.\nconst UI = Object.assign({\n  tipFront: 'Tap to flip',\n  tipBack: 'Left = Missed  \\u00b7  Right = Confident',\n  thirds: true,\n}, /*__UI__*/{});\nconst LADDER = [4, 8, 24];\nlet results = [];\nlet later = [];              // shaky cards waiting out their hour: {card, at}\nlet i = 0, flipped = false;\n// Where each card sits on the ladder, so the Confident button can say how\n// long it will be gone. Kept in step with the scheduler.\nlet steps = {};\nlet wakeTimer = null;\n\n/** Starts a fresh session on these cards. */\nfunction startSession(cards, practice) {\n  CARDS = cards.slice();\n  PRACTICE = !!practice;\n  results = [];\n  later = [];\n  i = 0;\n  steps = {};\n  CARDS.forEach(function (c) { steps[c.id] = c.step || 0; });\n  clearTimeout(wakeTimer);\n  document.querySelector('#done').style.display = 'none';\n  document.querySelector('#app').style.display = 'flex';\n  render();\n}\n\nconst $ = function (s) { return document.querySelector(s); };\n\n/** A shaky card whose hour is up goes back in at the front of the queue. */\nfunction releaseLater() {\n  const now = Date.now();\n  for (let k = later.length - 1; k >= 0; k--) {\n    if (later[k].at <= now) {\n      CARDS.splice(i, 0, later[k].card);\n      later.splice(k, 1);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/** \"Week 3\" for ECON 381; the unit's own name for classes organised by units. */\nfunction unitLabel(w) {\n  return UI.unitTitles && WEEKS[w - 1] ? WEEKS[w - 1].title : 'Week ' + w;\n}\n\nfunction setWeek(c) {\n  const w = WEEKS[c.week - 1];\n  const r = document.documentElement.style;\n  r.setProperty('--wk', w.color);\n  r.setProperty('--wk-soft', w.soft);\n}\n\nfunction chips(c) {\n  const w = WEEKS[c.week - 1];\n  let h = '<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">' + unitLabel(c.week) + '</span>';\n  if (c.starred) h += '<span class=\"chip star\">★ Tested</span>';\n  return h + '</div>';\n}\n\nfunction render() {\n  releaseLater();\n  const c = CARDS[i];\n  if (!c) return finishScreen();\n  flipped = false;\n  setWeek(c);\n  hidePeek();\n  $('#tip').textContent = UI.tipFront;\n  const doneCount = results.length;\n  $('#count').textContent = Math.min(i + 1, CARDS.length) + '/' + CARDS.length;\n  $('#fill').style.width = ((i / Math.max(CARDS.length, 1)) * 100) + '%';\n  $('#card').innerHTML = chips(c) +\n    '<div class=\"term\">' + c.term + '</div>' +\n    (UI.unitTitles ? '' : '<div class=\"weekname\">' + WEEKS[c.week - 1].title + '</div>');\n  $('#scroll').scrollTop = 0;\n  $('footer').style.visibility = 'hidden';\n}\n\nfunction flip() {\n  const c = CARDS[i];\n  if (!c) return;\n  flipped = !flipped;\n  if (!flipped) return render();\n  let h = chips(c) + '<div class=\"term sm\">' + c.term + '</div>';\n  h += '<div class=\"simple\">' + c.simple + '</div>';\n  if (c.formula) h += '<div class=\"fx\">' + c.formula + '</div>';\n  h += detail(c);\n  if (c.tags && c.tags.length) {\n    h += '<div class=\"tags\">' + c.tags.map(function (t) {\n      return '<span class=\"tag' + (/^PE/.test(t) ? ' pe' : '') + '\">' + esc(t) + '</span>';\n    }).join('') + '</div>';\n  }\n  $('#card').innerHTML = h;\n  $('#scroll').scrollTop = 0;\n  $('#tip').textContent = UI.tipBack;\n  const step = Math.min(steps[c.id] || 0, LADDER.length - 1);\n  $('#confHint').textContent = 'in ' + (LADDER[step] === 24 ? '1 day' : LADDER[step] + ' hours');\n  $('footer').style.visibility = 'visible';\n}\n\n/* ----------------------------------------------------------- answer detail */\n\nfunction section(title, body) {\n  if (!body) return '';\n  return '<div class=\"sect\"><h3>' + title + '</h3>' + body + '</div>';\n}\n\nfunction paras(x) {\n  if (!x) return '';\n  return (Array.isArray(x) ? x : [x]).map(function (p) { return '<p>' + p + '</p>'; }).join('');\n}\n\n/** The answer's small print, in a fixed order. A section with nothing to show\n *  is left out rather than shown empty. */\nfunction detail(c) {\n  let d = '';\n  d += section('Additional info', paras(c.more));\n  if (c.links && c.links.length) {\n    d += section('Connections', '<div class=\"links\">' + c.links.map(function (l) {\n      return '<span class=\"link\" data-peek=\"' + l.id + '\"><b>' + l.term + '</b></span>';\n    }).join('') + '</div>' + (c.connectNote ? '<p style=\"margin-top:8px\">' + c.connectNote + '</p>' : ''));\n  }\n  d += section('Real-world example', paras(c.example));\n  if (c.numbers && c.numbers.length) {\n    let rows = c.numbers.map(function (n) {\n      return '<div class=\"row\"><span class=\"lbl\">' + n[0] + '</span><span class=\"val\">' + n[1] + '</span></div>';\n    }).join('');\n    if (c.numbersSource) rows += '<div class=\"src\">' + c.numbersSource + '</div>';\n    d += section('By the numbers', rows);\n  }\n  if (c.played && c.played.length) {\n    d += section('Played out', '<ol class=\"steps\">' + c.played.map(function (s) {\n      return '<li>' + s + '</li>';\n    }).join('') + '</ol>');\n  }\n  if (c.graph) {\n    const g = drawGraph(c.graph);\n    if (g) d += section('Graph', '<div class=\"graph\">' + g.svg +\n      (g.cap ? '<div class=\"cap\">' + g.cap + '</div>' : '') + '</div>');\n  }\n  return d ? '<div class=\"detail\">' + d + '</div>' : '';\n}\n\nfunction drawGraph(spec) {\n  try {\n    const key = typeof spec === 'string' ? spec : spec.key;\n    const fn = window.GRAPHS && window.GRAPHS[key];\n    return fn ? fn(spec.args || {}) : null;\n  } catch (e) {\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------- connection peek */\n\nfunction showPeek(id) {\n  const c = CARDS[i];\n  const l = (c.links || []).filter(function (x) { return x.id === id; })[0];\n  if (!l) return;\n  const w = WEEKS[l.week - 1];\n  let h = '<div class=\"grab\"></div><div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\" style=\"background:' + w.soft +\n    ';color:' + w.color + '\">' + unitLabel(l.week) + '</span></div>' +\n    '<div class=\"term\" style=\"color:' + w.color + '\">' + l.term + '</div>' +\n    '<div class=\"simple\">' + l.simple + '</div>';\n  if (l.formula) h += '<div class=\"fx\">' + l.formula + '</div>';\n  h += '<div class=\"close\">Tap to close</div>';\n  $('#peek').innerHTML = h;\n  $('#peek').classList.add('on');\n}\n\nfunction hidePeek() { $('#peek').classList.remove('on'); }\nfunction peekOpen() { return $('#peek').classList.contains('on'); }\n\n/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- grading */\n\nfunction grade(rating) {\n  if (!flipped || !CARDS[i]) return;\n  const card = CARDS[i];\n  const t = Date.now();\n  // The next card isn't on screen for another moment; a second press in\n  // that gap must not grade it unseen.\n  flipped = false;\n  results.push({ id: card.id, rating: rating, t: t });\n  // Handed to Scriptable straight away so it is saved while the session is\n  // still open, not only on the way out. The sequence number is what the\n  // native side dedupes on: a card that comes round again is graded again.\n  ping('grade?id=' + encodeURIComponent(card.id) + '&rating=' + rating +\n       '&seq=' + (results.length - 1) + '&t=' + t);\n  const colors = { missed: '#C0392B', shaky: '#D9952B', confident: '#2E7D52' };\n  flash(rating.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + rating.slice(1), comesBack(card, rating), colors[rating]);\n\n  if (rating === 'missed') {\n    // Didn't know it: to the back of the deck, so it comes round again this\n    // session instead of disappearing.\n    steps[card.id] = 0;\n    CARDS.splice(i, 1);\n    CARDS.push(card);\n  } else if (rating === 'shaky') {\n    steps[card.id] = Math.max((steps[card.id] || 0) - 1, 0);\n    CARDS.splice(i, 1);\n    // In cram mode nothing is scheduled, so a shaky card simply goes back a\n    // few places; otherwise it waits out its hour.\n    if (PRACTICE) CARDS.splice(Math.min(i + 5, CARDS.length), 0, card);\n    else later.push({ card: card, at: t + 60 * 60 * 1000 });\n  } else {\n    steps[card.id] = Math.min((steps[card.id] || 0) + 1, LADDER.length - 1);\n    i++;\n  }\n  setTimeout(render, 170);\n}\n\n/** Fires an econ381:// request that the native side intercepts and blocks.\n *  Sent from a hidden iframe so the page itself never navigates. */\nfunction ping(path) {\n  if (window.ECON_HOST) { window.ECON_HOST.ping(path); return; }\n  const f = document.createElement('iframe');\n  f.style.display = 'none';\n  f.src = 'econ381://' + path;\n  document.body.appendChild(f);\n  setTimeout(function () { f.remove(); }, 80);\n}\n\n/** When a graded card will be seen again, said the moment it's graded, so\n *  the schedule is visible rather than taken on trust. Read before the\n *  card's step moves. */\nfunction comesBack(card, rating) {\n  if (PRACTICE) return rating === 'missed' ? 'again at the end' : rating === 'shaky' ? 'again in a few cards' : 'cram \\u00b7 not scheduled';\n  if (rating === 'missed') return 'again at the end of this session';\n  if (rating === 'shaky') return 'back in 1 hour';\n  const h = LADDER[Math.min(steps[card.id] || 0, LADDER.length - 1)];\n  return 'back in ' + (h === 24 ? '1 day' : h + ' hours');\n}\n\nlet flashTimer = null;\nfunction flash(text, sub, color) {\n  const f = $('#flash');\n  f.innerHTML = '<div>' + esc(text) + '<small>' + esc(sub) + '</small></div>';\n  f.style.background = color;\n  f.style.opacity = '.94';\n  clearTimeout(flashTimer);\n  flashTimer = setTimeout(function () { f.style.opacity = '0'; }, 520);\n}\n\nfunction finishScreen() {\n  $('#app').style.display = 'none';\n  $('#done').style.display = 'flex';\n  const last = {};\n  results.forEach(function (r) { last[r.id] = r.rating; });\n  const n = { missed: 0, shaky: 0, confident: 0 };\n  results.forEach(function (r) { n[r.rating]++; });\n  const seen = Object.keys(last).length;\n  $('#doneText').textContent = seen + ' card' + (seen === 1 ? '' : 's') + ' · ' +\n    results.length + ' answer' + (results.length === 1 ? '' : 's');\n  $('#tM').textContent = n.missed;\n  $('#tS').textContent = n.shaky;\n  $('#tC').textContent = n.confident;\n  if (later.length) {\n    const at = Math.min.apply(null, later.map(function (x) { return x.at; }));\n    const d = new Date(at);\n    let h = d.getHours(); const m = String(d.getMinutes()).padStart(2, '0');\n    const ap = h >= 12 ? 'pm' : 'am'; h = h % 12 || 12;\n    $('#laterText').textContent = later.length + ' shaky card' + (later.length === 1 ? '' : 's') +\n      ' come' + (later.length === 1 ? 's' : '') + ' back at ' + h + ':' + m + ap +\n      '. Leave this open and ' + (later.length === 1 ? 'it' : 'they') + '’ll reappear, or run the script again then.';\n    // Wait for them here as well: if the screen is left open, they appear.\n    wakeTimer = setTimeout(function wake() {\n      if (later.some(function (x) { return x.at <= Date.now(); })) {\n        $('#done').style.display = 'none';\n        $('#app').style.display = 'flex';\n        render();\n      } else wakeTimer = setTimeout(wake, 30000);\n    }, 30000);\n  } else {\n    $('#laterText').textContent = '';\n  }\n}\n\nfunction esc(s) {\n  return String(s == null ? '' : s).replace(/[&<>\"]/g, function (ch) {\n    return { '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '\"': '&quot;' }[ch];\n  });\n}\n\ndocument.addEventListener('click', function (e) {\n  if (peekOpen()) { hidePeek(); return; }\n  const pk = e.target.closest('[data-peek]');\n  if (pk) { showPeek(pk.dataset.peek); return; }\n  const act = e.target.closest('[data-act]');\n  if (!act) return;\n  if (!flipped) { flip(); return; }   // the buttons only grade after the reveal\n  grade(act.dataset.act);\n});\n\n/* The thirds are worked out from where the tap landed rather than from an\n   overlay, so the answer can scroll normally underneath. */\nconst scroll = $('#scroll');\nlet dragged = false;\nscroll.addEventListener('touchstart', function () { dragged = false; }, { passive: true });\nscroll.addEventListener('touchmove', function () { dragged = true; }, { passive: true });\nscroll.addEventListener('click', function (e) {\n  if (dragged) { dragged = false; return; }       // a tap that ended a scroll is not a grade\n  if (e.target.closest('[data-act],[data-peek]')) return;\n  if (peekOpen()) return;\n  if (!flipped || !UI.thirds) { flip(); return; }\n  const x = e.clientX / scroll.clientWidth;\n  if (x < 0.30) grade('missed');\n  else if (x > 0.70) grade('confident');\n  else flip();\n});\n\nif (CARDS.length) startSession(CARDS, PRACTICE);\n"};

const CARDS = DECK.cards;
const BY_ID = Object.fromEntries(CARDS.map((c) => [c.id, c]));
const WEEKS = DECK.weeks;

// --------------------------------------------------------------- storage

/** iCloud when it's on, so progress follows you between devices and
 *  survives re-pasting the script; the local folder when it isn't. */
function storage() {
  try {
    const fm = FileManager.iCloud();
    fm.documentsDirectory();
    return fm;
  } catch (e) {
    return FileManager.local();
  }
}

function progressPath(fm) {
  return fm.joinPath(fm.documentsDirectory(), PROGRESS_FILE);
}

async function readProgress(fm) {
  const p = progressPath(fm);
  try {
    if (!fm.fileExists(p)) return null;
    if (fm.isFileStoredIniCloud && fm.isFileStoredIniCloud(p) && !fm.isFileDownloaded(p)) {
      await fm.downloadFileFromiCloud(p);
    }
    const data = JSON.parse(fm.readString(p));
    return data && data.cards ? data : null;
  } catch (e) {
    return null;
  }
}

/** The iCloud copy, or the local one if a save ever had to fall back to it
 *  (whichever holds the more recent grade). */
async function loadProgress() {
  const latest = (d) => Math.max(0, ...Object.values(d.cards).map((s) => s.last || 0));
  const copies = [await readProgress(storage()), await readProgress(FileManager.local())]
    .filter(Boolean)
    .sort((a, b) => latest(b) - latest(a));
  return copies[0] || { cards: {} };
}

function saveProgress(progress) {
  const body = JSON.stringify(progress);
  try {
    const fm = storage();
    fm.writeString(progressPath(fm), body);
  } catch (e) {
    // iCloud refused the write: keep the session's grades locally rather
    // than losing them.
    const fm = FileManager.local();
    fm.writeString(progressPath(fm), body);
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ sync
//
// The same progress the website keeps, in the Supabase project the Arabic
// app (Wazn) uses. The phone signs in with the Wazn login it already keeps
// in the Keychain (wazn.email / wazn.password, shared with the Wazn
// scripts), pulls the website's progress, combines it with its own
// (mergeProgress: the latest grade per card wins) and pushes the result.
// Without a login or a connection everything still works from iCloud.

const SUPABASE_URL = "https://fphpcfecgnfoogfaeihu.supabase.co";
// Publishable key, meant to ship to apps; row-level security keeps each
// person's row private.
const SUPABASE_KEY = "sb_publishable_UFHFJ-b988nrZ_QP2AbQ4g_64Jq_5s2";
const KEY_EMAIL = "wazn.email";
const KEY_PASSWORD = "wazn.password";

async function credentials(promptIfMissing) {
  if (Keychain.contains(KEY_EMAIL) && Keychain.contains(KEY_PASSWORD)) {
    return { email: Keychain.get(KEY_EMAIL), password: Keychain.get(KEY_PASSWORD) };
  }
  if (!promptIfMissing) return null;
  const a = new Alert();
  a.title = "Sign in to sync";
  a.message = "Your Arabic app (Wazn) email and password. Kept in the iOS Keychain on this phone only.";
  a.addTextField("Email", "");
  a.addSecureTextField("Password");
  a.addAction("Sign in");
  a.addCancelAction("Cancel");
  if ((await a.presentAlert()) === -1) return null;
  const email = a.textFieldValue(0).trim(), password = a.textFieldValue(1);
  if (!email || !password) return null;
  Keychain.set(KEY_EMAIL, email);
  Keychain.set(KEY_PASSWORD, password);
  return { email, password };
}

async function signIn(promptIfMissing) {
  const creds = await credentials(promptIfMissing);
  if (!creds) return null;
  const req = new Request(SUPABASE_URL + "/auth/v1/token?grant_type=password");
  req.method = "POST";
  req.headers = { apikey: SUPABASE_KEY, "Content-Type": "application/json" };
  req.body = JSON.stringify({ email: creds.email, password: creds.password });
  let res = null;
  try { res = await req.loadJSON(); } catch (e) { return null; }   // offline
  if (!res || !res.access_token) {
    // A changed password: forget it so the next sign-in asks again.
    if (promptIfMissing) { Keychain.remove(KEY_EMAIL); Keychain.remove(KEY_PASSWORD); }
    return null;
  }
  return { token: res.access_token, userId: res.user && res.user.id };
}

function authHeaders(sess) {
  return { apikey: SUPABASE_KEY, Authorization: "Bearer " + sess.token, "Content-Type": "application/json" };
}

async function pullRemote(sess) {
  const req = new Request(SUPABASE_URL + "/rest/v1/" + COURSE.table + "?select=data" +
    (COURSE.course ? "&course_id=eq." + encodeURIComponent(COURSE.course) : ""));
  req.headers = authHeaders(sess);
  const rows = await req.loadJSON();
  if (!Array.isArray(rows)) throw new Error("couldn't read progress");
  return rows[0] ? rows[0].data : null;
}

/** Pull, combine, push: whatever the website graded comes in, and
 *  whatever the phone graded goes up. Returns whether it got through. */
async function syncNow(sess, progress) {
  if (!sess) return false;
  try {
    mergeProgress(progress, await pullRemote(sess));
    saveProgress(progress);
    const req = new Request(SUPABASE_URL + "/rest/v1/" + COURSE.table + "?on_conflict=" + (COURSE.course ? "user_id,course_id" : "user_id"));
    req.method = "POST";
    req.headers = Object.assign(authHeaders(sess), { Prefer: "resolution=merge-duplicates,return=minimal" });
    req.body = JSON.stringify(COURSE.course
      ? { user_id: sess.userId, course_id: COURSE.course, data: progress }
      : { user_id: sess.userId, data: progress });
    await req.load();
    const code = req.response && req.response.statusCode;
    return !code || code < 300;
  } catch (e) {
    return false;   // offline: iCloud has it, the next run syncs
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- widget

function timeOf(ms) {
  const d = new Date(ms);
  let h = d.getHours();
  const m = String(d.getMinutes()).padStart(2, "0");
  const ap = h >= 12 ? "pm" : "am";
  h = h % 12 || 12;
  return h + ":" + m + ap;
}

/** Plain text of a term, for places that can't render the HTML it may hold
 *  (subscripts in "Real GDP<sub>t</sub>" and the like). */
function plain(html) {
  return String(html || "")
    .replace(/<[^>]+>/g, "")
    .replace(/&amp;/g, "&")
    .replace(/&lt;/g, "<")
    .replace(/&gt;/g, ">")
    .replace(/&[a-z]+;/g, "");
}

function buildWidget(stats) {
  const w = new ListWidget();
  w.backgroundColor = new Color(CREAM);
  w.setPadding(14, 14, 12, 14);
  const big = config.widgetFamily === "medium" || config.widgetFamily === "large";

  const header = w.addStack();
  header.centerAlignContent();
  const title = header.addText(COURSE.code);
  title.font = Font.boldSystemFont(11);
  title.textColor = new Color(BRAND);
  header.addSpacer();
  const owed = stats.due + stats.newLeft;
  if (owed > 0) {
    const badge = header.addText(String(owed));
    badge.font = Font.boldSystemFont(13);
    badge.textColor = new Color(BRAND);
  }

  w.addSpacer(6);

  const card = stats.nextCard;
  if (!card) {
    const t = w.addText("All caught up");
    t.font = Font.semiboldSystemFont(15);
    t.textColor = new Color(INK);
    w.addSpacer(2);
    const s = w.addText(
      stats.upcoming < Infinity ? "Next card at " + timeOf(stats.upcoming) : "Nothing due right now.",
    );
    s.font = Font.systemFont(11);
    s.textColor = new Color(INK);
    s.textOpacity = 0.6;
  } else {
    const week = WEEKS[card.week - 1];
    const wk = w.addText((card.starred ? "★ " : "") + (COURSE.unitTitles ? week.title.toUpperCase() : "WEEK " + card.week));
    wk.font = Font.boldSystemFont(9);
    wk.textColor = new Color(week.color);
    w.addSpacer(2);

    const term = w.addText(plain(card.term));
    term.font = new Font("Georgia-Bold", big ? 24 : 19);
    term.textColor = new Color(INK);
    term.minimumScaleFactor = 0.55;
    term.lineLimit = 3;

    w.addSpacer(4);
    const parts = [];
    if (stats.due) parts.push(stats.due + " due");
    if (stats.newLeft) parts.push(stats.newLeft + " new");
    const hint = w.addText(parts.join(" · ") + " · tap to review");
    hint.font = Font.systemFont(10);
    hint.textColor = new Color(INK);
    hint.textOpacity = 0.6;
  }

  w.addSpacer();

  // Progress toward every card answered confidently at a full day's spacing.
  const bar = w.addStack();
  bar.size = new Size(0, 4);
  bar.cornerRadius = 2;
  bar.backgroundColor = new Color(INK, 0.09);
  const fillWidth = Math.round((big ? 300 : 130) * (stats.solid / stats.total));
  if (fillWidth > 0) {
    const fill = bar.addStack();
    fill.size = new Size(fillWidth, 4);
    fill.cornerRadius = 2;
    fill.backgroundColor = new Color(BRAND);
  }
  bar.addSpacer();
  w.addSpacer(3);
  const solid = w.addText(stats.solid + "/" + stats.total + " solid");
  solid.font = Font.systemFont(9);
  solid.textColor = new Color(INK);
  solid.textOpacity = 0.45;

  // Tapping anywhere runs this same script, which then falls into review mode.
  w.url = "scriptable:///run?scriptName=" + encodeURIComponent(Script.name());
  if (stats.due === 0 && stats.newLeft === 0 && stats.upcoming < Infinity) {
    w.refreshAfterDate = new Date(stats.upcoming + 60 * 1000);
  }
  return w;
}

// -------------------------------------------------------- review session

/** What the page needs about one card: the card itself plus the terms and
 *  one-line definitions of the cards it connects to, so a connection can be
 *  peeked at without leaving the session. */
function forPage(card) {
  const links = (card.connections || [])
    .map((id) => BY_ID[id])
    .filter(Boolean)
    .map((c) => ({ id: c.id, term: c.term, simple: c.simple, formula: c.formula || "", week: c.week }));
  return { ...card, links };
}

function reviewHTML(cards, mode) {
  const practice = mode === "cram";
  // Cards go in as JSON rather than templated into markup, and every "<" is
  // escaped so nothing in a card can close the script tag early.
  const json = (v) => JSON.stringify(v).replace(/</g, "\\u003c");
  const fill = (src, marker, value) => src.split(marker).join(value);

  let js = PAGE.js;
  js = fill(js, "/*__CARDS__*/[]", json(cards.map(forPage)));
  js = fill(js, "/*__WEEKS__*/[]", json(WEEKS));
  js = fill(js, "/*__PRACTICE__*/false", practice ? "true" : "false");
  if (COURSE.unitTitles) js = fill(js, "/*__UI__*/{}", json({ unitTitles: true }));
  let body = PAGE.body;
  body = fill(body, "__QUIT__", practice ? "Cram \u00b7 nothing is rescheduled" : "Swipe down when you\u2019re done");
  body = fill(body, "__DONE_ACTIONS__", '<p style="opacity:.45;font-size:13px">Swipe down to save and close.</p>');

  return '<!doctype html>\n<html><head><meta charset="utf-8">\n' +
    '<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no,viewport-fit=cover">\n' +
    "<style>\n" + PAGE.css + "</style></head>\n<body>\n" + body +
    "\n<script>\n" + GRAPH_SRC.replace(/<\/script/gi, "<\\/script") + "\n</script>\n<script>\n" + js + "</script>\n</body></html>";
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- session run

async function pick(title, message, options) {
  const a = new Alert();
  a.title = title;
  if (message) a.message = message;
  for (const o of options) a.addAction(o);
  a.addCancelAction("Cancel");
  return a.presentAlert();
}

async function tell(title, message) {
  const a = new Alert();
  a.title = title;
  a.message = message;
  a.addAction("OK");
  await a.presentAlert();
}

/** The start menu. Returns {cards, mode} or null if cancelled. */
async function chooseSession(progress, sess) {
  const now = Date.now();
  const st = summary(CARDS, progress, now);
  const starred = (c) => c.starred;
  const tested = buildQueue(CARDS, progress, now, starred, Infinity);
  const testedCount = tested.due.length + tested.fresh.length;

  const options = [];
  const actions = [];
  if (st.due + st.newLeft > 0) {
    const parts = [];
    if (st.due) parts.push(st.due + " due");
    if (st.newLeft) parts.push(st.newLeft + " new");
    options.push("Review " + parts.join(" + "));
    actions.push("review");
  }
  if (testedCount > 0) {
    options.push("★ Tested cards only (" + testedCount + ")");
    actions.push("tested");
  }
  options.push("One " + COURSE.unitWord.toLowerCase() + "…");
  actions.push("week");
  options.push("Cram all " + CARDS.length + " · no scheduling");
  actions.push("cram");
  options.push("Progress & reset…");
  actions.push("stats");
  if (!sess) {
    options.push("Sign in to sync with the website…");
    actions.push("signin");
  }

  let message = st.solid + " of " + st.total + " solid" + (sess ? " · synced" : "");
  if (st.due + st.newLeft === 0) {
    message = "All caught up" +
      (st.upcoming < Infinity ? " · next card at " + timeOf(st.upcoming) : "") +
      "\n" + message;
  }
  const choice = await pick(COURSE.title, message, options);
  if (choice === -1) return null;
  const act = actions[choice];

  if (act === "signin") return { signin: true };
  if (act === "review") {
    const q = buildQueue(CARDS, progress, now, null, st.newLeft);
    return { cards: interleave(q.due, q.fresh), mode: "review" };
  }
  if (act === "tested") {
    return { cards: interleave(tested.due, tested.fresh), mode: "review" };
  }
  if (act === "week") {
    const labels = WEEKS.map((w, k) => {
      const q = buildQueue(CARDS, progress, now, (c) => c.week === k + 1, Infinity);
      const n = q.due.length + q.fresh.length;
      return COURSE.unitWord + " " + (k + 1) + ": " + w.title + (n ? " (" + n + ")" : " ✓");
    });
    const unit = COURSE.unitWord.toLowerCase();
    const k = await pick("Which " + unit + "?", "Due and new cards from that " + unit + " only.", labels);
    if (k === -1) return null;
    const q = buildQueue(CARDS, progress, now, (c) => c.week === k + 1, Infinity);
    if (q.due.length + q.fresh.length === 0) {
      const again = await pick(
        COURSE.unitWord + " " + (k + 1) + " is caught up",
        "Nothing in it is due. Cram it anyway? That won't change its schedule.",
        ["Cram " + unit + " " + (k + 1)],
      );
      if (again === -1) return null;
      return { cards: shuffle(CARDS.filter((c) => c.week === k + 1)), mode: "cram" };
    }
    return { cards: interleave(q.due, q.fresh), mode: "review" };
  }
  if (act === "cram") {
    return { cards: shuffle(CARDS.slice()), mode: "cram" };
  }
  if (act === "stats") {
    await showStats(progress);
    return null;
  }
  return null;
}

async function showStats(progress) {
  const now = Date.now();
  const st = summary(CARDS, progress, now);
  const lines = WEEKS.map((w, k) => {
    const cs = CARDS.filter((c) => c.week === k + 1);
    const seen = cs.filter((c) => progress.cards[c.id]).length;
    const solid = cs.filter((c) => (progress.cards[c.id] || {}).solid).length;
    return COURSE.unitWord + " " + (k + 1) + ": " + seen + "/" + cs.length + " seen · " + solid + " solid";
  });
  // The cards missed most, worth a look in the notes.
  const hard = CARDS
    .map((c) => ({ c, s: progress.cards[c.id] }))
    .filter((x) => x.s && x.s.lapses > 0)
    .sort((a, b) => b.s.lapses - a.s.lapses)
    .slice(0, 5)
    .map((x) => "• " + plain(x.c.term) + " (" + x.s.lapses + "×)");
  const msg = lines.join("\n") +
    "\n\n" + st.unseen + " never seen · " + st.due + " due now" +
    (hard.length ? "\n\nMissed most:\n" + hard.join("\n") : "");

  const a = new Alert();
  a.title = st.solid + " of " + st.total + " solid";
  a.message = msg;
  a.addAction("OK");
  a.addDestructiveAction("Reset all progress");
  const choice = await a.presentAlert();
  if (choice === 1) {
    const sure = new Alert();
    sure.title = "Reset everything?";
    sure.message = "Every card goes back to new. This can't be undone.";
    sure.addDestructiveAction("Reset");
    sure.addCancelAction("Cancel");
    if ((await sure.presentAlert()) === 0) {
      const fresh = { cards: {}, days: progress.days || {}, resetAt: Date.now() };
      for (const k in progress) delete progress[k];
      Object.assign(progress, fresh);
      saveProgress(progress);
      await tell("Reset", "All " + CARDS.length + " cards are new again.");
    }
  }
}

async function runReview() {
  const progress = await loadProgress();
  let sess = await signIn(false);
  if (sess && !(await syncNow(sess, progress))) sess = null;
  let session = await chooseSession(progress, sess);
  while (session && session.signin) {
    sess = await signIn(true);
    if (sess && (await syncNow(sess, progress))) await tell("Synced", "This phone and the website now share progress.");
    else { sess = null; await tell("Couldn't sign in", "Check the email and password (the ones you use for Wazn) and your connection."); }
    session = await chooseSession(progress, sess);
  }
  if (!session || !session.cards.length) { if (sess) await syncNow(sess, progress); return; }
  const practice = session.mode === "cram";

  // The page shows "Confident · in N hours" from each card's ladder step.
  const cards = session.cards.map((c) => {
    const s = progress.cards[c.id];
    return { ...c, step: s ? s.step : 0 };
  });

  const applied = new Set();
  function apply(seq, id, rating, t) {
    if (practice || applied.has(seq) || !BY_ID[id]) return;
    if (!["missed", "shaky", "confident"].includes(rating)) return;
    progress.cards[id] = schedule(progress.cards[id], rating, t || Date.now());
    progress.days = progress.days || {};
    const day = isoDay(t || Date.now());
    progress.days[day] = (progress.days[day] || 0) + 1;
    applied.add(seq);
    try {
      saveProgress(progress);
    } catch (e) {
      applied.delete(seq);   // the pass after dismissal will try again
    }
  }

  const wv = new WebView();
  await wv.loadHTML(reviewHTML(cards, session.mode));

  // The page can't call into Scriptable mid-session, so it navigates to an
  // econ381:// URL and this handler intercepts it. Returning false blocks
  // the navigation and leaves the session on screen untouched.
  wv.shouldAllowRequest = (request) => {
    const url = String((request && request.url) || "");
    if (!url.startsWith("econ381://")) return true;
    if (url.startsWith("econ381://grade")) {
      const get = (k) => {
        const m = url.match(new RegExp("[?&]" + k + "=([^&]+)"));
        return m ? decodeURIComponent(m[1]) : null;
      };
      apply(Number(get("seq")), get("id"), get("rating"), Number(get("t")));
    }
    return false;
  };

  // present() resolves when the sheet is dismissed; the page is still alive
  // and its list of grades can be read back. Anything the live saves missed
  // is applied now, in order, so a session abandoned halfway still counts.
  await wv.present(true);

  let graded = [];
  try {
    const raw = await wv.evaluateJavaScript("JSON.stringify(results)", false);
    const parsed = JSON.parse(raw || "[]");
    if (Array.isArray(parsed)) graded = parsed;
  } catch (e) {
    /* nothing graded, or the page was gone */
  }
  graded.forEach((r, seq) => apply(seq, r.id, r.rating, r.t));
  if (sess && !practice) await syncNow(sess, progress);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ main

if (config.runsInWidget) {
  const progress = await loadProgress();
  try {
    const sess = await signIn(false);
    if (sess) { mergeProgress(progress, await pullRemote(sess)); saveProgress(progress); }
  } catch (e) { /* offline: the iCloud copy is fine */ }
  Script.setWidget(buildWidget(summary(CARDS, progress, Date.now())));
  Script.complete();
} else {
  await runReview();
  Script.complete();
}
